export const EP_MODS = [
  {
    id:1, num:"01", icon:"📍", free:true, dur:"2h 20m", lessons:6, color:"#1B4F72",
    title:"Overview & Epidemiology", sub:"Definition, Risk Factors, Pathophysiology & Classification by Site",
    aud:["doctor","nurse","pharmacist","labtech","student"],
    tagline:"A pregnancy in the wrong place is still a pregnancy - and understanding WHY implantation goes wrong, and WHERE, is the foundation for everything that follows.",
    story:`Emergency Department, Korle-Bu Teaching Hospital. Akosua Boateng, 27, presents with six weeks of amenorrhoea, a positive home pregnancy test, and two days of mild lower abdominal pain on the right side, with some light vaginal spotting.\n\n"I assumed this was just early pregnancy pain," she tells the triage midwife. "My friend said cramping is normal in early pregnancy."\n\nThe attending obstetrician, Dr. Mensah-Bonsu, reviews her history. "Some cramping CAN be normal," she explains, "but we need to confirm WHERE this pregnancy actually IS before we assume anything. A positive pregnancy test confirms pregnancy HORMONES are present - it does NOT confirm the pregnancy is in the UTERUS."\n\nAkosua looks puzzled. "Where else would it be?"\n\n"In about 1-2% of pregnancies," Dr. Mensah-Bonsu explains, "the fertilised egg implants OUTSIDE the uterine cavity - most commonly in the FALLOPIAN TUBE. This is called an ECTOPIC PREGNANCY - 'ectopic' simply means 'out of place.' The tube isn't designed to STRETCH the way the uterus is, so as the pregnancy grows, it can cause PAIN, and in some cases, the tube can RUPTURE - causing INTERNAL BLEEDING, which can be LIFE-THREATENING."\n\n"I had a tubal infection a few years ago," Akosua mentions. "Could that be related?"\n\n"That's VERY relevant," says Dr. Mensah-Bonsu. "Previous tubal infection - often from sexually transmitted infections - is one of the STRONGEST risk factors for ectopic pregnancy, because it can damage the delicate lining of the tube, affecting how the fertilised egg travels through it."\n\n"We're going to do some blood tests and an ultrasound scan to find out EXACTLY where this pregnancy is. This is one of the situations in medicine where finding out QUICKLY matters enormously - even if you're feeling relatively well right now."\n\nThis module is the foundation - understanding WHAT ectopic pregnancy is, WHY it happens, WHERE it can occur, and WHO is at higher risk.`,
    sections:[
      {
        h:"📖 Definition, Epidemiology & Normal Implantation",
        a:`Think of the journey of a fertilised egg as a commute - from the fallopian tube (where fertilisation usually occurs) to the uterus (the intended 'destination' for implantation), a journey that normally takes about 3-4 days. An ECTOPIC PREGNANCY occurs when the egg implants ANYWHERE along this route OTHER than its intended destination - most commonly, it simply doesn't complete the commute and implants WITHIN the fallopian tube itself.`,
        c:`DEFINITION:\nAn ectopic pregnancy is a pregnancy that implants OUTSIDE the endometrial cavity of the uterus. The term 'ectopic' (Greek: 'out of place') describes the LOCATION, not the viability or hormonal activity of the pregnancy - an ectopic pregnancy is HORMONALLY ACTIVE (produces beta-hCG, can cause a positive pregnancy test, and may even produce early symptoms similar to normal pregnancy) but is NOT VIABLE in the long term and poses a risk to the person carrying it.\n\nEPIDEMIOLOGY:\n→ Ectopic pregnancy occurs in approximately 1-2% of ALL pregnancies, though rates vary by population and risk factor prevalence\n→ It remains a SIGNIFICANT cause of MATERNAL MORTALITY in early pregnancy, particularly in settings where access to early diagnosis (ultrasound, beta-hCG testing) and emergency care is LIMITED - making this a topic of PARTICULAR relevance in resource-constrained settings, including parts of Ghana and the broader sub-Saharan African region\n→ The INCIDENCE of ectopic pregnancy has been INCREASING in many settings over recent decades - attributed to factors including increased rates of pelvic inflammatory disease (PID), increased use of assisted reproductive technology (which carries its own ectopic pregnancy risk), and IMPROVED DIAGNOSTIC CAPABILITY (detecting ectopics that might previously have gone undiagnosed or been misdiagnosed)\n\nNORMAL IMPLANTATION - THE 'COMMUTE':\n→ Fertilisation typically occurs in the AMPULLA of the fallopian tube (the widest part)\n→ The fertilised egg (developing into a BLASTOCYST) is transported toward the uterus over approximately 3-4 days, propelled by TUBAL CILIARY ACTION and MUSCULAR CONTRACTIONS of the tube\n→ Implantation in the UTERINE ENDOMETRIUM normally occurs around 6-7 days after fertilisation\n\nPATHOPHYSIOLOGY - WHY DOES THE 'COMMUTE' FAIL?\nMost ectopic pregnancies result from a DELAY or DISRUPTION in this transport process, such that the blastocyst implants BEFORE reaching the uterine cavity. Key contributing mechanisms:\n→ DAMAGE TO TUBAL CILIA/MUSCULATURE: scarring from infection (most notably PID, often related to Chlamydia trachomatis or Neisseria gonorrhoeae) can damage the delicate ciliated lining and alter normal tubal motility, SLOWING or HALTING transport\n→ ANATOMICAL ABNORMALITIES: congenital tubal abnormalities, or ACQUIRED abnormalities from previous surgery (e.g. tubal ligation, reversal, or other pelvic/tubal surgery)\n→ ALTERED TUBAL ENVIRONMENT: smoking has been associated with altered tubal motility (affecting ciliary function) - an important MODIFIABLE risk factor\n→ ASSISTED REPRODUCTIVE TECHNOLOGY (ART): in vitro fertilisation (IVF) carries its OWN ectopic pregnancy risk, related both to underlying tubal factors that may have led to the need for IVF in the first place, AND potentially to the embryo transfer process itself`,
        kp:[
          "Ectopic pregnancy = implantation outside the endometrial cavity, occurring in ~1-2% of pregnancies - hormonally active but not viable long-term and potentially life-threatening",
          "A positive pregnancy test confirms pregnancy hormones (beta-hCG) are present but does NOT confirm the pregnancy's LOCATION",
          "Normal implantation: fertilisation in the tubal ampulla, ~3-4 day transport to the uterus, implantation in the endometrium around day 6-7",
          "Ectopic pregnancy results from delayed/disrupted transport - most often due to tubal damage from PID (Chlamydia/gonorrhoea), prior tubal surgery, smoking (altered ciliary function), or IVF-related factors",
          "Ectopic pregnancy is a significant cause of early-pregnancy maternal mortality, particularly where access to early diagnosis and emergency care is limited"
        ],
        callouts:[
          {role:"student", type:"exam", text:"A frequently tested concept: 'A positive pregnancy test does NOT confirm the pregnancy is intrauterine.' This single statement underlies the ENTIRE diagnostic approach to early pregnancy with pain/bleeding - the question is ALWAYS 'where is this pregnancy?' before assuming anything about viability. Exam questions often present a positive pregnancy test PLUS pain/bleeding and ask for the NEXT step - the answer is virtually always further investigation to LOCATE the pregnancy (ultrasound +/- serial beta-hCG), not reassurance."},
          {role:"doctor", type:"clinical", text:"Any patient of reproductive age presenting with abdominal/pelvic pain should have a PREGNANCY TEST as part of initial assessment, REGARDLESS of stated contraceptive use, sexual history, or perceived likelihood of pregnancy - patients may be unaware of pregnancy, contraceptive failure occurs, and the CONSEQUENCES of missing an ectopic pregnancy (rupture, haemorrhage, death) are severe enough that this LOW-COST, RAPID test should be ROUTINE in this clinical scenario."},
          {role:"pharmacist", type:"clinical", text:"When dispensing emergency contraception or counselling on regular contraception, it's worth noting that NO contraceptive method (including tubal ligation/sterilisation) provides ABSOLUTE protection against ectopic pregnancy if pregnancy DOES occur despite the method - in fact, SOME methods (e.g. previous tubal surgery/sterilisation) are themselves RISK FACTORS for ectopic pregnancy IF pregnancy occurs. Patients who become pregnant after tubal sterilisation should be counselled that ECTOPIC PREGNANCY is a possibility requiring prompt assessment."},
          {role:"nurse", type:"clinical", text:"When taking a history from a patient with early pregnancy symptoms plus pain or bleeding, SPECIFICALLY ask about: previous PID/sexually transmitted infections, previous ectopic pregnancy (a strong risk factor for RECURRENCE), previous tubal/pelvic surgery, smoking status, and method of conception (spontaneous vs assisted reproduction). These targeted questions directly inform the CLINICAL SUSPICION level and urgency of further investigation."},
          {role:"labtech", type:"practical", text:"A QUALITATIVE positive urine or serum pregnancy test (detecting beta-hCG above a threshold) confirms PREGNANCY but provides NO information about LOCATION or VIABILITY. When a QUANTITATIVE serum beta-hCG is requested in the context of suspected ectopic pregnancy, RAPID turnaround is clinically important, as the VALUE (and, even more so, the TREND over 48 hours - covered in Module 2) directly informs time-sensitive management decisions."},
        ]
      },
      {
        h:"🗺️ Classification by Anatomical Site & Risk Factors",
        a:`If the fallopian tube is the most common 'wrong stop' on the fertilised egg's commute, it's worth knowing that there are SEVERAL possible 'wrong stops' - each with its own implications for presentation, risk, and management. Think of this section as a MAP of all the possible ectopic locations, from the most common (and relatively more predictable) to the rarer, higher-risk variants that will be revisited in Module 5.`,
        c:`CLASSIFICATION BY ANATOMICAL SITE:\n\n1. TUBAL ECTOPIC PREGNANCY (by far the MOST COMMON - approximately 95% of ectopic pregnancies):\n→ AMPULLARY (most common tubal site, ~70% of tubal ectopics) - the WIDEST part of the tube, allows somewhat MORE 'room' before symptoms/rupture compared to narrower sites\n→ ISTHMIC (~12% of tubal ectopics) - the NARROWER part of the tube closer to the uterus - tends to present EARLIER with pain/rupture due to LESS space for the pregnancy to grow\n→ FIMBRIAL/INFUNDIBULAR (~6-11%) - near the tube's OPEN end, closest to the ovary\n→ INTERSTITIAL (~2-3%) - within the MUSCULAR WALL of the uterus itself, where the tube enters the uterus - this is a HIGHER-RISK variant (covered further in Module 5) due to PROXIMITY TO THE UTERINE BLOOD SUPPLY, meaning rupture can cause MORE SEVERE haemorrhage, and may present LATER (more room to grow within the muscular wall) before becoming apparent\n\n2. OVARIAN ECTOPIC PREGNANCY (~1-3%):\n→ Implantation on or within the OVARY itself - relatively RARE, can be difficult to distinguish from a tubal ectopic or corpus luteal cyst on imaging\n\n3. CERVICAL ECTOPIC PREGNANCY (RARE, <1%):\n→ Implantation within the CERVICAL CANAL - a HIGHER-RISK variant (Module 5) given proximity to the cervix's rich vascular supply and the challenges of management in this location\n\n4. CAESAREAN SCAR ECTOPIC PREGNANCY (RARE but INCREASINGLY RECOGNISED):\n→ Implantation within the scar tissue of a PREVIOUS CAESAREAN SECTION - increasingly recognised as caesarean section rates have risen globally - represents a DISTINCT, HIGHER-RISK variant (Module 5) given the scar's compromised tissue integrity\n\n5. ABDOMINAL ECTOPIC PREGNANCY (VERY RARE):\n→ Implantation within the ABDOMINAL/PERITONEAL CAVITY, outside the reproductive organs entirely - EXTREMELY RARE, and in even RARER cases, has been reported to progress to a point of fetal viability with delivery via laparotomy, though this represents an EXCEPTIONAL and high-risk scenario, not a 'safe alternative' to normal pregnancy\n\n6. HETEROTOPIC PREGNANCY:\n→ The COEXISTENCE of an INTRAUTERINE pregnancy AND an ectopic pregnancy SIMULTANEOUSLY - RARE in SPONTANEOUS conception, but with INCREASED incidence in ASSISTED REPRODUCTION (where multiple embryos may be transferred) - covered further in Module 5\n\nRISK FACTORS - SUMMARY:\n→ STRONGEST RISK FACTORS: previous ectopic pregnancy (recurrence risk significantly elevated), previous tubal surgery (including sterilisation), documented tubal pathology/damage\n→ SIGNIFICANT RISK FACTORS: history of PID/sexually transmitted infections (particularly Chlamydia trachomatis), use of assisted reproductive technology (IVF)\n→ MODERATE RISK FACTORS: smoking (dose-related effect on tubal ciliary function), increasing maternal age, multiple previous sexual partners (proxy for STI exposure risk)\n→ INTRAUTERINE DEVICE (IUD) IN SITU: IUDs are HIGHLY effective at preventing pregnancy OVERALL - but IF pregnancy occurs DESPITE an IUD, there is a PROPORTIONALLY HIGHER LIKELIHOOD that pregnancy is ECTOPIC (because the IUD is MORE effective at preventing INTRAUTERINE implantation than at preventing ALL pregnancy) - an important nuance: IUDs do NOT INCREASE the ABSOLUTE risk of ectopic pregnancy compared to NO contraception, but pregnancy occurring WITH an IUD in place should raise SUSPICION for ectopic location\n→ NO IDENTIFIABLE RISK FACTORS: importantly, a SIGNIFICANT PROPORTION of ectopic pregnancies occur in patients with NO IDENTIFIABLE RISK FACTORS - absence of risk factors should NOT lower clinical suspicion when SYMPTOMS (pain, bleeding with positive pregnancy test) are present`,
        kp:[
          "~95% of ectopic pregnancies are TUBAL, with ampullary being most common (~70%); isthmic ectopics tend to present EARLIER due to less space",
          "Interstitial, cervical, and caesarean scar ectopics are RARER but HIGHER-RISK variants due to proximity to major blood supplies (Module 5)",
          "Heterotopic pregnancy (simultaneous intrauterine + ectopic) is rare spontaneously but more common with assisted reproduction",
          "Strongest risk factors: previous ectopic pregnancy, previous tubal surgery/damage; significant factors: PID/Chlamydia history, IVF; moderate: smoking, age",
          "IUD in situ: doesn't increase ABSOLUTE ectopic risk vs no contraception, but pregnancy occurring DESPITE an IUD should raise suspicion for ectopic location - and a significant proportion of ectopics occur with NO identifiable risk factors"
        ],
        callouts:[
          {role:"student", type:"exam", text:"A commonly tested distinction: 'Why might an ISTHMIC ectopic present EARLIER than an AMPULLARY ectopic?' Answer: the isthmus is the NARROWEST part of the tube, providing LESS room for the pregnancy to grow before causing significant pain or rupture - compared to the wider ampulla. This 'anatomy determines timing of presentation' logic is a useful PRINCIPLE applicable across the different ectopic sites discussed in this module."},
          {role:"doctor", type:"clinical", text:"The IUD nuance is FREQUENTLY MISUNDERSTOOD by patients (and sometimes clinicians): an IUD does NOT 'cause' ectopic pregnancy in an ABSOLUTE sense - it's HIGHLY effective overall. But if a patient WITH an IUD presents with a POSITIVE PREGNANCY TEST, this should INCREASE (not decrease) suspicion for ECTOPIC location specifically, because the IUD's PROTECTIVE EFFECT is GREATER against intrauterine implantation than against tubal implantation. This is an important, CLINICALLY ACTIONABLE point to communicate clearly."},
          {role:"pharmacist", type:"clinical", text:"When a patient with a documented history of PID, previous ectopic pregnancy, or previous tubal surgery presents to a pharmacy with a POSITIVE HOME PREGNANCY TEST and ANY abdominal pain or bleeding, this represents an OPPORTUNITY for IMPORTANT SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE: given their ELEVATED risk profile, PROMPT medical assessment (rather than 'wait and see' approaches sometimes appropriate for LOW-risk early pregnancy symptoms) should be ENCOURAGED, with clear guidance on WHERE to seek this assessment urgently."},
          {role:"nurse", type:"clinical", text:"When documenting a history for a patient with suspected early pregnancy complications, RECORD SPECIFICALLY whether an IUD is/was in place, details of ANY assisted reproduction (including whether MULTIPLE embryos were transferred - relevant to heterotopic pregnancy risk), and the SPECIFIC SITE of any PREVIOUS ectopic pregnancy if applicable (as this may inform which tube/site is of GREATEST concern this time, though recurrence can occur in either tube or the contralateral side)."},
          {role:"labtech", type:"practical", text:"While LABORATORY TESTING itself doesn't directly determine the ANATOMICAL SITE of an ectopic pregnancy (this is primarily an IMAGING question, covered in Module 2), understanding the FULL CLINICAL CONTEXT (e.g. 'this is a patient with a history of bilateral tubal surgery presenting with a positive pregnancy test') helps you appreciate WHY certain patients are flagged for URGENT/PRIORITY processing of beta-hCG samples, even when their CURRENT clinical presentation might otherwise seem 'routine early pregnancy.'"},
        ]
      },
    ],
    ev:"RCOG Green-top Guideline on Ectopic Pregnancy and Miscarriage; Williams Obstetrics; WHO Recommendations on Maternal Health; NICE Guideline on Ectopic Pregnancy and Miscarriage"
  },
  {
    id:2, num:"02", icon:"🩺", free:false, dur:"2h 30m", lessons:6, color:"#922B21",
    title:"Clinical Presentation & Diagnosis", sub:"Symptoms, beta-hCG Trends, Ultrasound & Differential Diagnosis",
    aud:["doctor","nurse","pharmacist","labtech","student"],
    tagline:"From a vague ache to a haemorrhagic emergency - ectopic pregnancy can present along a wide spectrum, and the diagnostic journey often hinges on a single number tracked over 48 hours.",
    story:`Early Pregnancy Assessment Unit, Greater Accra Regional Hospital. Adwoa Owusu, 31, is six weeks pregnant by her last menstrual period, with mild lower abdominal discomfort and spotting - feeling generally well, walking and talking normally.\n\nThe midwife, Sister Asante, takes a careful history and explains: "Your symptoms COULD represent several things - a normal early pregnancy with some implantation spotting, a miscarriage beginning, OR an ectopic pregnancy. At THIS stage, with mild symptoms, we often CAN'T tell the difference just by examining you - we need to TRACK some numbers."\n\nA blood test shows a beta-hCG of 800 mIU/mL. An ultrasound scan doesn't clearly show a pregnancy in the uterus OR clearly identify an ectopic location - this is called a 'pregnancy of unknown location.'\n\n"This is actually a COMMON situation," Sister Asante reassures her. "At a beta-hCG of 800, a NORMAL early intrauterine pregnancy might not YET be visible on ultrasound - it could still be TOO EARLY. We need to repeat this blood test in 48 HOURS and see how the number CHANGES."\n\nTwo days later, Adwoa returns. Her repeat beta-hCG is 850 - only a SMALL increase, far LESS than the roughly DOUBLING expected in a healthy early intrauterine pregnancy over 48 hours.\n\n"This pattern - a beta-hCG that's RISING, but much MORE SLOWLY than expected, or sometimes PLATEAUING - is CONCERNING for an ECTOPIC pregnancy, even though we STILL can't see it directly on ultrasound yet," explains Dr. Acheampong, who has joined the consultation. "It doesn't tell us EXACTLY where the pregnancy is - but it tells us this is UNLIKELY to be a NORMAL, viable intrauterine pregnancy, and an ECTOPIC location is a LEADING possibility that we need to actively investigate and monitor closely, given the risks if it IS ectopic and continues to grow."\n\nThis module is about that diagnostic journey - the SPECTRUM of presentations, the POWER (and LIMITATIONS) of beta-hCG trends, and the imaging and differential diagnosis that complete the picture.`,
    sections:[
      {
        h:"⚠️ Clinical Presentation — From Subtle Symptoms to Haemorrhagic Emergency",
        a:`Ectopic pregnancy can present along a SPECTRUM - from a patient who feels almost entirely WELL with only mild symptoms (as in Adwoa's story), to a patient who arrives in PROFOUND SHOCK from a RUPTURED ectopic causing massive internal bleeding. Recognising WHERE on this spectrum a patient sits - and that the spectrum can SHIFT RAPIDLY - is one of the most important skills in managing this condition.`,
        c:`THE 'CLASSIC TRIAD' - AND ITS LIMITATIONS:\nThe traditionally described triad of ectopic pregnancy is:\n1. AMENORRHOEA (missed period) with a POSITIVE PREGNANCY TEST\n2. ABDOMINAL/PELVIC PAIN - often UNILATERAL (one-sided), though can be CENTRAL or BILATERAL, and may be CONSTANT or INTERMITTENT/COLICKY\n3. VAGINAL BLEEDING - typically LIGHT, often described as 'spotting' or DARK/BROWN ('prune juice'-like) - though can occasionally be HEAVIER\n\nCRITICALLY: this 'classic triad' is PRESENT IN ONLY A MINORITY of patients - MANY patients present with ONLY ONE or TWO of these features, or with ATYPICAL symptoms entirely. RELYING on the FULL TRIAD to 'rule in' or 'rule out' ectopic pregnancy is a RECOGNISED DIAGNOSTIC PITFALL.\n\nADDITIONAL/ATYPICAL PRESENTATIONS:\n→ SHOULDER TIP PAIN: pain referred to the SHOULDER, classically associated with DIAPHRAGMATIC IRRITATION from blood/free fluid in the abdomen (a sign of significant INTRA-ABDOMINAL BLEEDING) - this is an IMPORTANT 'red flag' symptom that may seem UNRELATED to pregnancy at first glance\n→ DIZZINESS/SYNCOPE: may indicate SIGNIFICANT BLOOD LOSS (internal haemorrhage) even WITHOUT obvious external bleeding\n→ GI SYMPTOMS: nausea, vomiting, diarrhoea, or urge to defecate - can occur due to PERITONEAL IRRITATION from blood in the pelvis, and may lead to MISDIAGNOSIS as a primarily GI condition (e.g. gastroenteritis)\n→ CERVICAL/ADNEXAL TENDERNESS on examination - though examination findings can be SUBTLE or absent, particularly EARLY\n\nTHE HAEMODYNAMICALLY UNSTABLE/RUPTURED ECTOPIC - A TRUE EMERGENCY:\nWhen an ectopic pregnancy RUPTURES (most classically a tubal ectopic causing the tube to rupture, though other sites can also bleed significantly), the presentation can shift RAPIDLY to:\n→ SEVERE, often SUDDEN-ONSET abdominal pain\n→ SIGNS OF HYPOVOLAEMIC SHOCK: tachycardia, hypotension, pallor, dizziness/collapse, signs of poor peripheral perfusion\n→ PERITONITIC ABDOMEN: rigid, tender abdomen with signs of peritoneal irritation\n→ SHOULDER TIP PAIN (as above) may be prominent\n→ This represents a TIME-CRITICAL SURGICAL EMERGENCY (Module 6) - RAPID recognition and ESCALATION is essential, as DELAY directly contributes to MATERNAL MORTALITY\n\nA CRITICAL TEACHING POINT - THE 'WELL-LOOKING' PATIENT:\nAs illustrated by Adwoa's case, MANY patients with ectopic pregnancy - INCLUDING some who will LATER deteriorate - INITIALLY appear WELL, with NORMAL VITAL SIGNS and only MILD symptoms. The ABSENCE of 'emergency' features at a SINGLE POINT IN TIME does NOT EXCLUDE ectopic pregnancy, and does NOT guarantee the patient will REMAIN STABLE - ONGOING MONITORING and SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE (what symptoms should prompt IMMEDIATE return) are ESSENTIAL parts of management for patients being investigated as 'pregnancy of unknown location.'`,
        kp:[
          "The 'classic triad' (amenorrhoea, pain, bleeding) is present in only a MINORITY of patients - relying on it to rule in/out ectopic pregnancy is a recognised pitfall",
          "Shoulder tip pain is an important 'red flag' suggesting diaphragmatic irritation from intra-abdominal blood/free fluid",
          "GI symptoms (nausea, vomiting, urge to defecate) can occur from peritoneal irritation and may lead to misdiagnosis as a GI condition",
          "A ruptured ectopic presents as a hypovolaemic shock/peritonitic emergency - severe sudden pain, tachycardia, hypotension, pallor - a time-critical surgical emergency",
          "Many patients (including some who later deteriorate) initially appear WELL with normal vitals - absence of emergency features doesn't exclude ectopic pregnancy or guarantee stability, making safety-netting advice essential"
        ],
        callouts:[
          {role:"student", type:"exam", text:"Shoulder tip pain in a patient with a positive pregnancy test is a HIGH-YIELD exam 'red flag' - the underlying mechanism (diaphragmatic irritation from haemoperitoneum, via referred pain along the phrenic nerve distribution, C3-C5, which shares dermatomal overlap with the shoulder) is worth understanding, not just memorising as a fact. A question describing 'pregnancy test positive + shoulder pain' is testing whether you recognise this as a sign of SIGNIFICANT INTRA-ABDOMINAL BLEEDING, not a musculoskeletal issue."},
          {role:"doctor", type:"clinical", text:"For ANY patient of reproductive age presenting with ACUTE ABDOMINAL PAIN, GI symptoms, or even ISOLATED SHOULDER PAIN without obvious cause, a PREGNANCY TEST should be considered as part of the INITIAL workup if there's ANY possibility of pregnancy - the COST of this test is minimal compared to the CONSEQUENCE of MISSING a ruptured ectopic in a patient initially triaged toward a GI or musculoskeletal differential."},
          {role:"pharmacist", type:"clinical", text:"If a patient presents to a pharmacy describing early pregnancy symptoms PLUS ANY of: significant pain, dizziness, fainting, or shoulder pain - this represents an URGENT REFERRAL situation, REGARDLESS of how 'well' the patient appears at that moment. Pharmacists are often a FIRST POINT OF CONTACT and should have a LOW THRESHOLD for urgent referral to emergency obstetric/gynaecological services when these symptoms combine with early pregnancy."},
          {role:"nurse", type:"clinical", text:"When a patient is being managed as 'pregnancy of unknown location' (as in Adwoa's case) and discharged for outpatient follow-up/repeat bloods, CLEAR, WRITTEN SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE is ESSENTIAL: explicitly list symptoms that should prompt IMMEDIATE return (worsening pain, especially severe/sudden; heavy bleeding; dizziness/fainting; shoulder tip pain), and ensure the patient has CLEAR INSTRUCTIONS on HOW and WHERE to access emergency care if these occur, INCLUDING outside normal clinic hours."},
          {role:"labtech", type:"practical", text:"In a HAEMODYNAMICALLY UNSTABLE patient with suspected ruptured ectopic, laboratory priorities shift to URGENT: FULL BLOOD COUNT (assessing for significant blood loss - though HAEMOGLOBIN may not have DROPPED yet in VERY ACUTE bleeding, as haemodilution takes time), GROUP AND SAVE/CROSSMATCH (anticipating possible TRANSFUSION need - Module 6), and COAGULATION studies if MASSIVE haemorrhage is a concern. RAPID communication of these results directly supports TIME-CRITICAL surgical decision-making."},
        ]
      },
      {
        h:"📈 Beta-hCG Trends, Ultrasound Diagnosis & Differential Diagnosis",
        a:`If a SINGLE beta-hCG value is like a SINGLE PHOTOGRAPH, TRACKING beta-hCG over 48 hours is like comparing TWO PHOTOGRAPHS taken two days apart - the CHANGE between them tells a story that NEITHER photograph alone could tell. Combined with ULTRASOUND (which has its OWN 'can I see it yet?' limitations depending on hCG levels and gestational age), this is how the diagnostic puzzle of 'pregnancy of unknown location' gets solved.`,
        c:`SERUM BETA-hCG - THE 48-HOUR TREND:\n→ In a NORMAL, VIABLE intrauterine pregnancy, beta-hCG typically RISES by AT LEAST 63% (often described as 'roughly doubling,' though the precise minimum expected rise has been refined in various studies) over 48 HOURS, in the EARLY stages of pregnancy\n→ A RISE SLOWER than expected, a PLATEAU, or a FALL in beta-hCG over 48 hours is CONCERNING and suggests the pregnancy is NOT developing normally - this pattern can occur with BOTH a FAILING intrauterine pregnancy (miscarriage) AND an ECTOPIC pregnancy - the TREND ALONE cannot DISTINGUISH between these two possibilities, which is why it's COMBINED with ultrasound findings and clinical context\n→ A FALLING beta-hCG generally suggests a FAILING pregnancy (either intrauterine miscarriage OR a 'resolving'/failing ectopic) - though the RATE of fall and ABSOLUTE values still inform further management\n\nTHE 'DISCRIMINATORY ZONE':\n→ This refers to the beta-hCG level ABOVE WHICH a NORMAL intrauterine pregnancy would be EXPECTED to be VISIBLE on TRANSVAGINAL ULTRASOUND\n→ Historically often cited around 1500-2000 mIU/mL (varying somewhat by guideline/institution and ultrasound equipment quality)\n→ If beta-hCG is ABOVE this zone and NO intrauterine pregnancy is visualised on ultrasound, this raises SIGNIFICANT CONCERN for ECTOPIC pregnancy (though a recently FAILED early intrauterine pregnancy, e.g. a very early miscarriage, can also produce this pattern)\n→ If beta-hCG is BELOW this zone, the ABSENCE of a visible intrauterine pregnancy is LESS DEFINITIVE - it may simply be 'TOO EARLY' for a NORMAL pregnancy to be visible yet - hence the need for SERIAL TESTING (as in Adwoa's case)\n\nTRANSVAGINAL ULTRASOUND FINDINGS:\n→ DEFINITIVE INTRAUTERINE PREGNANCY: visualisation of a GESTATIONAL SAC WITH A YOLK SAC and/or FETAL POLE WITHIN THE UTERINE CAVITY - this is REASSURING for intrauterine location, though does NOT entirely exclude the (rare) possibility of HETEROTOPIC pregnancy (Module 5) if risk factors are present\n→ 'PSEUDOSAC': a collection of fluid/blood WITHIN the uterine cavity that can SOMETIMES MIMIC a gestational sac on ultrasound, occurring in SOME ectopic pregnancies due to hormonal effects on the endometrium - DISTINGUISHING a true gestational sac from a pseudosac requires CAREFUL ultrasound assessment (e.g. looking for a yolk sac/fetal pole, assessing sac characteristics) - a recognised source of DIAGNOSTIC ERROR if not approached carefully\n→ ADNEXAL MASS: an ADNEXAL (near the ovary/tube) mass SEPARATE from the ovary itself, particularly with the characteristic 'BAGEL SIGN' or 'RING OF FIRE' (on Doppler) appearance, raises SIGNIFICANT SUSPICION for tubal ectopic pregnancy - though NOT always definitively visualised, particularly EARLY\n→ FREE FLUID in the pelvis/abdomen: may represent BLOOD (haemoperitoneum) - particularly significant if ECHOGENIC (suggesting blood rather than simple fluid) and/or if FOUND in larger amounts or in the ABDOMEN (not just pelvis) - raises concern for RUPTURE or significant tubal bleeding\n→ 'PREGNANCY OF UNKNOWN LOCATION' (PUL): the term used when beta-hCG is POSITIVE but ultrasound does NOT clearly show an intrauterine pregnancy, ectopic pregnancy, OR retained products of conception - requires SERIAL MONITORING (beta-hCG trends +/- repeat ultrasound) to CLARIFY\n\nDIFFERENTIAL DIAGNOSIS - WHAT ELSE COULD THIS BE?:\n→ MISCARRIAGE (failing intrauterine pregnancy): can present SIMILARLY (pain, bleeding) with SIMILAR beta-hCG trend patterns - ultrasound findings (presence/absence of intrauterine pregnancy, products of conception) help DISTINGUISH, though OVERLAP and 'PUL' scenarios occur\n→ OVARIAN CYST (including CORPUS LUTEUM CYST, which is a NORMAL feature of early pregnancy but can cause pain, especially if it RUPTURES or undergoes TORSION) - ultrasound helps distinguish, though a corpus luteum cyst CAN be mistaken for an ectopic-related mass by INEXPERIENCED ultrasound operators\n→ OVARIAN TORSION: can cause SEVERE, often SUDDEN unilateral pain - may coexist WITH early pregnancy and requires its OWN urgent recognition\n→ APPENDICITIS: particularly relevant given that ATYPICAL ectopic presentations (Section 1) can MIMIC appendicitis (right-sided pain, GI symptoms) - and CONVERSELY, appendicitis can occur DURING pregnancy and be misattributed to pregnancy-related causes\n→ PELVIC INFLAMMATORY DISEASE (PID): can cause pelvic pain and, if a patient is ALSO pregnant (less common but possible), distinguishing PID-related pain from ectopic-related pain requires careful assessment - though IMPORTANTLY, a NEGATIVE pregnancy test would make PID (rather than ectopic) the relevant diagnosis for pelvic pain\n→ URINARY TRACT INFECTION/RENAL COLIC: can cause lower abdominal/flank pain that may be considered in the differential, distinguished by urinalysis findings and pain characteristics, ALONGSIDE pregnancy test results`,
        kp:[
          "Normal early intrauterine pregnancy: beta-hCG rises ~63%+ over 48 hours; slower rise/plateau/fall is concerning but does NOT by itself distinguish failing intrauterine pregnancy from ectopic - combine with ultrasound",
          "Discriminatory zone (~1500-2000 mIU/mL): above this, absence of visible intrauterine pregnancy on TVUS raises significant ectopic concern; below this, 'too early to see' remains possible",
          "Pseudosac (intrauterine fluid mimicking a gestational sac) is a recognised pitfall - look for true yolk sac/fetal pole to confirm intrauterine pregnancy",
          "Adnexal mass with 'bagel sign'/'ring of fire,' plus free fluid (especially echogenic/abdominal), raises suspicion for tubal ectopic and possible rupture",
          "Key differentials: miscarriage, ovarian cyst (including corpus luteum)/torsion, appendicitis, PID, UTI/renal colic - pregnancy test result is the key branch point distinguishing pregnancy-related from non-pregnancy causes"
        ],
        callouts:[
          {role:"student", type:"exam", text:"A classic exam scenario presents a beta-hCG value, an ultrasound finding (or lack thereof), and asks for the diagnosis/next step. KEY LOGIC: beta-hCG ABOVE discriminatory zone + NO intrauterine pregnancy seen = HIGH suspicion ectopic (or recently failed very early IUP). beta-hCG BELOW discriminatory zone + NO intrauterine pregnancy seen = 'pregnancy of unknown location,' requiring SERIAL beta-hCG (48-hour repeat) rather than immediate definitive diagnosis. Mixing these up is a common error - the discriminatory zone is the KEY THRESHOLD that changes the INTERPRETATION of an 'empty' ultrasound."},
          {role:"doctor", type:"clinical", text:"The PSEUDOSAC pitfall has REAL clinical consequences - if a pseudosac is MISTAKEN for a true intrauterine gestational sac, an ECTOPIC pregnancy could be MISSED (false reassurance of 'intrauterine pregnancy seen'). CAREFUL ultrasound assessment - ideally by an experienced operator, looking SPECIFICALLY for a YOLK SAC or FETAL POLE (which a pseudosac lacks) rather than just 'a sac-like structure' - is essential, and when DOUBT exists, treating the situation as 'pregnancy of unknown location' with FOLLOW-UP rather than premature reassurance is the SAFER approach."},
          {role:"pharmacist", type:"clinical", text:"Patients undergoing 'pregnancy of unknown location' monitoring often experience SIGNIFICANT ANXIETY during the 48-hour wait between blood tests - while pharmacists don't make the diagnosis, providing CLEAR, ACCURATE information about WHY this waiting period is necessary (the beta-hCG TREND, not a single value, provides the needed information) and REINFORCING safety-netting advice (when to seek urgent care DURING this waiting period) can be a valuable supportive role."},
          {role:"nurse", type:"clinical", text:"When a patient describes RIGHT-SIDED abdominal pain with NAUSEA/VOMITING and is of reproductive age, ENSURE a pregnancy test is performed and the RESULT is known BEFORE a diagnosis of 'probable appendicitis' (or similar) is finalised - the OVERLAP in presentation between ectopic pregnancy and appendicitis means this single test result can SIGNIFICANTLY change the diagnostic and management pathway, and should not be OMITTED or DELAYED due to assumptions based on the presenting complaint alone."},
          {role:"labtech", type:"practical", text:"For SERIAL beta-hCG monitoring (the 48-hour trend), CONSISTENCY in TESTING METHOD/ASSAY matters - results from DIFFERENT assays or laboratories may not be DIRECTLY COMPARABLE due to differences in calibration/reference standards. Where possible, serial samples for the SAME patient's PUL monitoring should be processed using the SAME ASSAY, and if a patient has had testing at a DIFFERENT facility, this should be NOTED when interpreting trends, as an apparent 'change' between different assays may not accurately reflect the TRUE biological trend."},
        ]
      },
    ],
    ev:"RCOG Green-top Guideline on Ectopic Pregnancy and Miscarriage; NICE Guideline NG126; Williams Obstetrics; ESHRE Guideline on Diagnosis and Management of Ectopic Pregnancy"
  },
  {
    id:3, num:"03", icon:"💊", free:false, dur:"2h 30m", lessons:6, color:"#0B5345",
    title:"Medical Management", sub:"Methotrexate — Mechanism, Eligibility, Protocols & Monitoring",
    aud:["doctor","nurse","pharmacist","labtech","student"],
    tagline:"For carefully selected patients, a single injection can resolve an ectopic pregnancy without surgery - but 'carefully selected' is the key phrase, and the monitoring that follows is just as important as the injection itself.",
    story:`Gynaecology Outpatient Clinic, 37 Military Hospital. Following Adwoa's story from Module 2, her beta-hCG trend and a subsequent ultrasound have now identified a SMALL tubal ectopic pregnancy on the right side, measuring approximately 2.5cm, with NO significant free fluid and a beta-hCG of 1400 mIU/mL. Adwoa remains CLINICALLY WELL, with stable vital signs.\n\n"Given how things look," explains Dr. Acheampong, "you may be a CANDIDATE for MEDICAL management - a medication called METHOTREXATE, given as an INJECTION, instead of surgery."\n\n"How does an injection treat this?" Adwoa asks.\n\n"Methotrexate stops RAPIDLY DIVIDING CELLS from multiplying," Dr. Acheampong explains. "The cells of the developing pregnancy (the trophoblast) are RAPIDLY DIVIDING - methotrexate interferes with this, causing the pregnancy tissue to STOP GROWING and gradually be REABSORBED by your body, WITHOUT needing surgery."\n\n"Is it right for everyone?" Adwoa asks.\n\n"No - and this is IMPORTANT," says Dr. Acheampong. "Methotrexate is suitable when the ectopic is SMALL, your beta-hCG is BELOW certain thresholds, there's NO significant bleeding or pain suggesting rupture, and you're CLINICALLY STABLE - which describes YOUR situation. It's NOT suitable if there's evidence of RUPTURE, if the ectopic is VERY large, if beta-hCG is VERY high, or if there's a FETAL HEARTBEAT visible - in those situations, SURGERY (Module 4) is needed instead."\n\n"If you proceed with methotrexate," she continues, "this is NOT a 'one injection and done' situation - you'll need REPEATED blood tests over the following days and weeks to confirm your beta-hCG is FALLING as expected, and you'll need to AVOID certain things - alcohol, certain vitamins, and you absolutely CANNOT become pregnant again until we confirm this has fully resolved, given methotrexate's effects."\n\nThis module covers methotrexate - HOW it works, WHO it's right for, the PROTOCOLS used, and the MONITORING and SAFETY considerations that follow.`,
    sections:[
      {
        h:"⚗️ Methotrexate — Mechanism, Eligibility Criteria & Contraindications",
        a:`Methotrexate is like hitting the 'pause and reverse' button on rapidly dividing cells - by blocking a key enzyme needed for cells to make new DNA, it stops the rapidly dividing trophoblastic cells of the ectopic pregnancy from continuing to grow, allowing the body to gradually reabsorb the tissue. But this 'pause button' affects ANY rapidly dividing cells in the body - which is WHY careful patient selection and monitoring are essential.`,
        c:`MECHANISM OF ACTION:\n→ Methotrexate is a FOLATE ANTAGONIST - it inhibits DIHYDROFOLATE REDUCTASE (DHFR), an enzyme required to convert dihydrofolate to TETRAHYDROFOLATE, which is essential for SYNTHESISING the building blocks of DNA (purines and pyrimidines)\n→ By blocking DHFR, methotrexate PREVENTS DNA SYNTHESIS, which PREFERENTIALLY affects RAPIDLY DIVIDING CELLS - including the TROPHOBLASTIC cells of the ectopic pregnancy\n→ This causes the trophoblastic tissue to STOP PROLIFERATING and undergo INVOLUTION (shrinkage/breakdown), with the products gradually REABSORBED by the body over subsequent WEEKS\n→ IMPORTANTLY: methotrexate does NOT cause IMMEDIATE resolution - the process of beta-hCG decline and tissue resolution occurs GRADUALLY, which is WHY ongoing MONITORING (Section 2) is essential, and why patients must understand this is NOT an 'instant fix'\n\nELIGIBILITY CRITERIA FOR METHOTREXATE (MEDICAL MANAGEMENT):\nGenerally, methotrexate is considered appropriate when ALL of the following are present:\n→ HAEMODYNAMICALLY STABLE patient with NO signs of ACTIVE BLEEDING/RUPTURE\n→ NO SIGNIFICANT PAIN (or pain that is mild/controlled)\n→ ECTOPIC PREGNANCY SIZE typically BELOW a certain threshold (commonly cited around 3.5-4cm, though specific thresholds vary by guideline)\n→ BETA-hCG BELOW a certain threshold (commonly cited around 1500-5000 mIU/mL depending on the specific protocol/guideline and whether single-dose or multi-dose regimens are used - HIGHER beta-hCG levels are associated with LOWER success rates with methotrexate)\n→ NO FETAL CARDIAC ACTIVITY visible on ultrasound (presence of fetal cardiac activity significantly REDUCES methotrexate success rates and may be considered a RELATIVE or ABSOLUTE contraindication depending on guidelines)\n→ ABLE AND WILLING to COMPLY with REQUIRED FOLLOW-UP (serial blood tests, avoiding certain medications/substances, avoiding pregnancy for a specified period afterward)\n\nCONTRAINDICATIONS TO METHOTREXATE:\n→ HAEMODYNAMIC INSTABILITY or evidence of RUPTURE/significant haemoperitoneum - these patients require SURGERY (Module 4/6), NOT medical management\n→ HETEROTOPIC PREGNANCY where the INTRAUTERINE pregnancy is DESIRED/viable - methotrexate would HARM the intrauterine pregnancy too (it doesn't 'selectively' target only the ectopic location) - this is a CRITICAL consideration in heterotopic pregnancy management (Module 5)\n→ BREASTFEEDING - methotrexate is EXCRETED in breast milk and is CONTRAINDICATED during breastfeeding\n→ IMMUNODEFICIENCY/significant IMMUNOCOMPROMISE\n→ ACTIVE PULMONARY DISEASE (relative contraindication in some contexts)\n→ PEPTIC ULCER DISEASE\n→ SIGNIFICANT HEPATIC or RENAL IMPAIRMENT (methotrexate is metabolised/excreted via these systems, and impairment increases TOXICITY risk)\n→ SIGNIFICANT HAEMATOLOGICAL ABNORMALITIES (e.g. significant cytopenias) - BASELINE BLOODS (full blood count, renal function, liver function, blood group) are REQUIRED before treatment\n→ KNOWN SENSITIVITY to methotrexate`,
        kp:[
          "Methotrexate inhibits dihydrofolate reductase (DHFR), blocking DNA synthesis - preferentially affects rapidly dividing trophoblastic cells, causing GRADUAL involution over weeks, not immediate resolution",
          "Eligibility generally requires: haemodynamic stability, no significant pain/rupture signs, ectopic size below ~3.5-4cm, beta-hCG below threshold (~1500-5000 depending on protocol), NO fetal cardiac activity, and ability to comply with follow-up",
          "Higher beta-hCG and presence of fetal cardiac activity both REDUCE methotrexate success rates",
          "Contraindications include haemodynamic instability/rupture (needs surgery), heterotopic pregnancy with a desired intrauterine pregnancy, breastfeeding, immunocompromise, hepatic/renal impairment, peptic ulcer disease, significant cytopenias",
          "Baseline bloods (FBC, renal/liver function, blood group) are required before treatment given methotrexate's effects on rapidly dividing cells and excretion pathways"
        ],
        callouts:[
          {role:"student", type:"exam", text:"A frequently tested 'why' question: 'Why does the presence of FETAL CARDIAC ACTIVITY reduce the likelihood of methotrexate success?' The underlying concept: cardiac activity generally indicates a MORE ADVANCED/larger pregnancy with potentially MORE robust trophoblastic tissue/vascularity - methotrexate is more likely to be EFFECTIVE against SMALLER, LESS established trophoblastic tissue. This connects the MECHANISM (affecting rapidly dividing cells) to the CLINICAL ELIGIBILITY CRITERIA (size/cardiac activity thresholds) - exam questions often test this LINKING of mechanism to clinical decision-making."},
          {role:"doctor", type:"clinical", text:"BASELINE INVESTIGATIONS before methotrexate (FBC, renal function/creatinine, liver function tests, blood group and antibody screen, and confirmation of beta-hCG and ultrasound findings) are NOT merely 'routine' - each has a SPECIFIC PURPOSE: renal/liver function assess methotrexate CLEARANCE/TOXICITY risk, FBC assesses BASELINE cytopenia risk (methotrexate can cause myelosuppression), and blood group informs ANTI-D REQUIREMENTS (Module 6) if the patient is Rh-negative, REGARDLESS of whether medical or surgical management is chosen."},
          {role:"pharmacist", type:"clinical", text:"When dispensing or counselling on methotrexate for ectopic pregnancy, a CRITICAL counselling point is AVOIDANCE of FOLATE-CONTAINING SUPPLEMENTS (including most PRENATAL VITAMINS, which contain folic acid) DURING treatment - since methotrexate works by ANTAGONISING folate metabolism, folate supplementation could REDUCE its effectiveness. Patients should be advised to STOP prenatal vitamins/folic acid supplements as directed, and to AVOID ALCOHOL and NSAIDs (which can affect methotrexate clearance/increase toxicity risk) during treatment."},
          {role:"nurse", type:"clinical", text:"When a patient is being CONSIDERED for methotrexate, ENSURE they understand and can VERBALISE the KEY POINTS: this is NOT an immediate fix (resolution occurs over WEEKS with monitoring), they must AVOID PREGNANCY for the period specified by their team (commonly at least 3 MONTHS, though specific guidance should come from the treating team, given methotrexate's potential effects on FUTURE pregnancies if conception occurred too soon after treatment), and they must understand the SAFETY-NETTING for symptoms that would suggest TREATMENT FAILURE/ONGOING RISK OF RUPTURE (since methotrexate does NOT guarantee the ectopic won't still rupture during the resolution process - Section 2)."},
          {role:"labtech", type:"practical", text:"BASELINE FULL BLOOD COUNT, RENAL FUNCTION (creatinine/eGFR), and LIVER FUNCTION TESTS before methotrexate administration are TIME-SENSITIVE - results are needed to CONFIRM ELIGIBILITY (e.g. ADEQUATE renal/hepatic function for safe methotrexate clearance, ABSENCE of significant baseline cytopenia) BEFORE the medication is given. Delays in processing these baseline samples can delay TREATMENT INITIATION for a patient who may be ANXIOUS about their diagnosis and eager to proceed with an agreed management plan."},
        ]
      },
      {
        h:"📋 Treatment Protocols & Post-Treatment Monitoring",
        a:`If methotrexate's mechanism is a 'pause and reverse button,' the MONITORING that follows is like watching a video in SLOW MOTION to confirm the 'reverse' is actually happening as expected - because methotrexate doesn't provide INSTANT feedback, beta-hCG trends over the following DAYS and WEEKS are the primary way clinicians confirm SUCCESS, or identify EARLY that ADDITIONAL treatment (a second dose, or a switch to surgery) may be needed.`,
        c:`METHOTREXATE PROTOCOLS - SINGLE-DOSE vs MULTI-DOSE:\n\n1. SINGLE-DOSE PROTOCOL (more commonly used):\n→ A SINGLE intramuscular dose, calculated based on BODY SURFACE AREA (a common dosing reference is approximately 50mg/m² body surface area, though SPECIFIC dosing should follow LOCAL/CURRENT PROTOCOLS)\n→ Beta-hCG is measured on DAY 4 and DAY 7 after the injection\n→ A DECREASE of AT LEAST ~15% between DAY 4 and DAY 7 is generally considered an INDICATOR OF SUCCESS (a NORMAL initial RISE or PLATEAU between day 1-4 can occur and does NOT necessarily indicate failure - this initial pattern is EXPECTED in some cases before the decline begins)\n→ If the DAY 4-7 decline is INADEQUATE (<~15%), a SECOND DOSE of methotrexate may be given, with the monitoring schedule RESTARTING from that point\n→ If beta-hCG continues to be INADEQUATE after a SECOND dose, or if at ANY point the patient becomes SYMPTOMATIC/UNSTABLE, SURGICAL management (Module 4) is indicated\n\n2. MULTI-DOSE (e.g. 'two-dose' or alternating-day) PROTOCOLS:\n→ Used in SOME settings/for SOME patients (e.g. with HIGHER baseline beta-hCG) - involves MULTIPLE doses on ALTERNATING days, sometimes WITH 'rescue' folinic acid (leucovorin) doses to REDUCE methotrexate TOXICITY while maintaining efficacy against the trophoblastic tissue (folinic acid 'rescues' NORMAL cells more than it rescues the TARGETED trophoblastic tissue, in the doses/timing used)\n→ SPECIFIC protocols vary by institution/guideline - the KEY CONCEPT is that MULTI-DOSE approaches exist as an ALTERNATIVE/escalation, particularly for patients at the HIGHER end of eligible beta-hCG ranges\n\nONGOING MONITORING UNTIL RESOLUTION:\n→ AFTER initial success is confirmed (adequate day 4-7 decline), beta-hCG is typically monitored WEEKLY until it reaches a NON-PREGNANT level (often defined as below a specific threshold, e.g. <5-15 mIU/mL depending on the assay) - this can take SEVERAL WEEKS\n→ Throughout this period, the patient remains at SOME ongoing (though REDUCED) risk of TUBAL RUPTURE while the tissue is being reabsorbed - SAFETY-NETTING advice (Module 2's 'red flags': severe pain, signs of significant bleeding, dizziness/syncope, shoulder tip pain) remains ESSENTIAL throughout this monitoring period, NOT just before treatment\n\nSIDE EFFECTS & TOXICITY MONITORING:\n→ COMMON, GENERALLY MILD/SELF-LIMITING side effects: abdominal pain (can be DIFFICULT to distinguish from 'treatment working as expected' vs 'concerning pain suggesting rupture' - a recognised CLINICAL CHALLENGE requiring careful assessment), nausea, stomatitis (mouth ulcers/soreness), mild transient liver enzyme elevation, mild gastrointestinal upset\n→ LESS COMMON but MORE SERIOUS: significant MYELOSUPPRESSION (bone marrow suppression - affecting blood cell production), significant HEPATOTOXICITY, PNEUMONITIS (rare) - these are MORE LIKELY with MULTI-DOSE regimens or in patients with BASELINE risk factors (renal/hepatic impairment) - REPEAT bloods (FBC, renal/liver function) may be monitored, particularly with multi-dose regimens or if the patient develops concerning symptoms\n\nWHAT 'FAILURE' OF METHOTREXATE MEANS - AND WHAT HAPPENS NEXT:\n→ 'Failure' can mean: INADEQUATE beta-hCG decline (requiring a second dose or switch to surgery), or DEVELOPMENT of symptoms/signs suggesting RUPTURE during the monitoring period (requiring URGENT surgical assessment, REGARDLESS of beta-hCG trend at that point)\n→ IMPORTANTLY: the OVERALL SUCCESS RATE of appropriately-selected single-dose methotrexate is GENERALLY HIGH (often cited in the region of 70-95% depending on the population/beta-hCG levels), but patients should understand that SURGERY remains a POSSIBLE OUTCOME if methotrexate is NOT successful or if RUPTURE occurs during monitoring - this should be discussed as part of INFORMED CONSENT before starting medical management`,
        kp:[
          "Single-dose protocol (most common): IM dose based on body surface area; beta-hCG measured day 4 and day 7; a decline of ~15%+ between day 4-7 indicates success; an initial rise/plateau days 1-4 can be NORMAL and doesn't necessarily mean failure",
          "Inadequate decline → second dose possible; continued inadequate response or instability/symptoms → switch to SURGERY",
          "Multi-dose protocols (sometimes with folinic acid 'rescue') exist as alternatives/escalation, particularly for higher beta-hCG levels",
          "After initial success, weekly beta-hCG monitoring continues until non-pregnant levels - the patient remains at SOME ongoing rupture risk during this period, so safety-netting advice continues throughout",
          "Common side effects (abdominal pain, nausea, stomatitis) can be hard to distinguish from concerning symptoms; serious toxicity (myelosuppression, hepatotoxicity) is monitored via repeat bloods, especially with multi-dose regimens"
        ],
        callouts:[
          {role:"student", type:"exam", text:"A commonly tested 'trap' question describes a patient on methotrexate whose beta-hCG ROSE between day 1 and day 4, and asks whether this indicates FAILURE. The CORRECT answer: NOT NECESSARILY - an initial RISE or PLATEAU in the FIRST FEW DAYS can be a NORMAL, EXPECTED pattern before decline begins, and the KEY ASSESSMENT POINT is the DAY 4 to DAY 7 trend, not day 1 to day 4. This tests whether you understand the SPECIFIC TIMING built into the monitoring protocol, rather than assuming 'any rise = bad' at any point."},
          {role:"doctor", type:"clinical", text:"Distinguishing 'EXPECTED' abdominal pain (from the methotrexate's effect on the trophoblastic tissue, sometimes called 'separation pain') from pain suggesting IMPENDING/ACTUAL RUPTURE is a GENUINE CLINICAL CHALLENGE without a simple algorithm. Factors that should INCREASE concern for rupture INCLUDE: pain that is SEVERE, WORSENING, or associated with HAEMODYNAMIC CHANGES (tachycardia, hypotension), SHOULDER TIP PAIN, or SIGNS OF PERITONISM on examination - in such cases, URGENT REASSESSMENT (including repeat ultrasound/bloods and consideration of surgical review) is warranted, REGARDLESS of the beta-hCG trend at that point in the protocol."},
          {role:"pharmacist", type:"clinical", text:"For patients on methotrexate, REINFORCE that NSAIDs (e.g. ibuprofen, diclofenac) should generally be AVOIDED for pain relief during treatment, as NSAIDs can affect RENAL CLEARANCE of methotrexate, potentially INCREASING toxicity risk - PARACETAMOL is generally the PREFERRED analgesic option in this context. This is a PRACTICAL, ACTIONABLE counselling point that directly affects patient SAFETY during the monitoring period."},
          {role:"nurse", type:"clinical", text:"For patients attending for SERIAL beta-hCG monitoring after methotrexate, each visit is an OPPORTUNITY to REASSESS symptoms (not just draw blood) - specifically ask about pain CHARACTER/SEVERITY changes, any bleeding changes, dizziness, or shoulder pain since the LAST visit. This ONGOING SYMPTOM REVIEW, alongside the beta-hCG trend, contributes to the OVERALL assessment of whether treatment is proceeding as expected or whether ESCALATION is needed."},
          {role:"labtech", type:"practical", text:"For patients on methotrexate protocols, beta-hCG results on DAY 4 and DAY 7 (and subsequent WEEKLY monitoring) are TIME-CRITICAL for clinical decision-making (whether a SECOND DOSE is needed, whether the patient can be REASSURED of expected progress, or whether ESCALATION is required) - and for MULTI-DOSE regimens or patients with risk factors, ACCOMPANYING FBC/renal/liver function results support TOXICITY MONITORING. Flagging these as PRIORITY samples, with results communicated PROMPTLY to the requesting team, directly supports time-sensitive protocol decisions."},
        ]
      },
    ],
    ev:"RCOG Green-top Guideline on Ectopic Pregnancy and Miscarriage; NICE Guideline NG126; ESHRE Guideline on Diagnosis and Management of Ectopic Pregnancy; ACOG Practice Bulletin on Tubal Ectopic Pregnancy"
  },
  {
    id:4, num:"04", icon:"🔪", free:false, dur:"2h 30m", lessons:6, color:"#7D3C98",
    title:"Surgical Management", sub:"Salpingectomy vs Salpingostomy, Approach Selection & Fertility Implications",
    aud:["doctor","nurse","pharmacist","labtech","student"],
    tagline:"When medical management isn't appropriate or hasn't worked, surgery resolves the immediate problem - but the CHOICE of operation carries implications that extend well beyond the operating theatre.",
    story:`Theatre Suite, Komfo Anokye Teaching Hospital. Yaa Asantewaa Mensah, 24, was admitted overnight with worsening pelvic pain and a beta-hCG of 8500 mIU/mL - too high for methotrexate to be considered appropriate, with ultrasound confirming a 4cm left tubal ectopic pregnancy and a small amount of free fluid in the pelvis. She remains haemodynamically stable but is in significant discomfort.\n\n"We're recommending LAPAROSCOPIC SURGERY," explains Mr. Adjei, the consulting gynaecologist, "using keyhole techniques rather than a large incision, given you're currently stable."\n\n"What will you actually DO?" Yaa asks.\n\n"There are TWO main options," Mr. Adjei explains. "SALPINGECTOMY - removing the AFFECTED TUBE entirely, along with the ectopic pregnancy. Or SALPINGOSTOMY - making a small incision IN the tube to REMOVE the pregnancy while LEAVING the tube itself in place."\n\n"Which is better?" Yaa asks.\n\n"It depends on several things," Mr. Adjei explains. "Your OTHER tube - the right one - appears HEALTHY on today's scan. If your OTHER tube is healthy, SALPINGECTOMY of the AFFECTED tube is GENERALLY preferred - it's a DEFINITIVE solution for THIS pregnancy, with LOWER risk of needing FURTHER treatment afterward, and your remaining healthy tube can still support future pregnancies."\n\n"But if this WERE your only functioning tube - say, if your right tube had previously been removed or was significantly damaged - SALPINGOSTOMY, attempting to PRESERVE this tube, might be considered MORE STRONGLY, even though it carries its OWN risks, including the possibility that NOT ALL the pregnancy tissue is removed, requiring FOLLOW-UP beta-hCG monitoring similar to methotrexate, and in SOME cases, additional treatment."\n\n"Given your right tube looks healthy on today's scan, salpingectomy of the affected left tube is our RECOMMENDATION - but this is YOUR decision, and we'll discuss the FULL implications for FUTURE FERTILITY before you decide."\n\nThis module covers the surgical approaches, HOW the choice between them is made, and what each means for the future.`,
    sections:[
      {
        h:"🔬 Salpingectomy vs Salpingostomy — Choosing the Approach",
        a:`Think of the affected fallopian tube after an ectopic pregnancy as a damaged section of road. SALPINGECTOMY is like CLOSING that section of road PERMANENTLY and rerouting all traffic via the OTHER route (the remaining tube) - a DEFINITIVE solution, appropriate when an ALTERNATIVE route exists and is in GOOD CONDITION. SALPINGOSTOMY is like REPAIRING the damaged section IN PLACE - preserving the route, but with some RISK that the repair isn't COMPLETE, requiring FOLLOW-UP inspection.`,
        c:`SALPINGECTOMY:\n→ COMPLETE REMOVAL of the AFFECTED FALLOPIAN TUBE, along with the ectopic pregnancy contained within it\n→ GENERALLY PREFERRED when:\n  → The CONTRALATERAL (other side) tube is PRESENT and appears HEALTHY/functional\n  → The AFFECTED tube is SIGNIFICANTLY DAMAGED (e.g. due to the ectopic itself, or PRE-EXISTING damage that may have CONTRIBUTED to this ectopic occurring, and could increase risk of a FUTURE ectopic in the SAME tube if preserved)\n  → There is SIGNIFICANT BLEEDING from the tube that would be DIFFICULT to control with a tube-preserving approach\n  → The patient has COMPLETED childbearing or has NO DESIRE for future fertility via that side (though this is rarely the SOLE deciding factor, as IVF remains an option even with NO tubes)\n→ ADVANTAGES: DEFINITIVE treatment of THIS ectopic - essentially ELIMINATES the risk of PERSISTENT TROPHOBLAST (retained pregnancy tissue continuing to produce beta-hCG, requiring further treatment - see salpingostomy below) from THIS episode, and ELIMINATES the risk of a FUTURE ectopic occurring in THIS SAME (removed) tube\n→ CONSIDERATIONS: future fertility relies on the REMAINING tube (if present and functional) for SPONTANEOUS conception, OR on ASSISTED REPRODUCTION (IVF) if needed - REMOVAL of one tube does NOT mean a patient CANNOT conceive again, but DOES mean conception must occur via the REMAINING tube (or via IVF, bypassing tubes entirely)\n\nSALPINGOSTOMY (also called salpingotomy):\n→ A SMALL INCISION is made IN the fallopian tube, and the ectopic pregnancy tissue is REMOVED from WITHIN the tube, while the TUBE ITSELF is LEFT IN PLACE (the incision may be left to heal without sutures, or closed, depending on technique/surgeon preference)\n→ GENERALLY CONSIDERED when:\n  → The CONTRALATERAL tube is ABSENT, SIGNIFICANTLY DAMAGED, or PREVIOUSLY REMOVED - making PRESERVATION of THIS tube of HIGHER IMPORTANCE for future SPONTANEOUS fertility\n  → The patient strongly desires TUBAL PRESERVATION and understands the associated risks/follow-up requirements\n→ KEY RISK - PERSISTENT TROPHOBLAST: NOT ALL trophoblastic tissue may be successfully removed during salpingostomy - RESIDUAL tissue can CONTINUE to produce beta-hCG and potentially CONTINUE to grow, requiring FOLLOW-UP beta-hCG monitoring SIMILAR to the methotrexate protocol (Module 3) - if beta-hCG does NOT decline appropriately after salpingostomy, ADDITIONAL TREATMENT (methotrexate, or further surgery) may be needed\n→ FUTURE ECTOPIC RISK: the PRESERVED tube, having ALREADY been the site of ONE ectopic (and often having UNDERLYING damage that CONTRIBUTED to this), carries an ELEVATED risk of a FUTURE ectopic occurring in the SAME tube, compared to a tube that has NEVER had an ectopic\n\nTHE 'BOTH TUBES DAMAGED/ABSENT' SCENARIO:\nIf BOTH tubes are absent or significantly compromised (e.g. due to PREVIOUS surgery on both sides, or extensive bilateral disease), the DISCUSSION shifts significantly - SALPINGOSTOMY to attempt preservation of REMAINING tubal tissue, EVEN with its risks, may be considered MORE STRONGLY, alongside EARLY discussion of IVF as a future option REGARDLESS of which surgical approach is taken for THIS ectopic - this represents a SIGNIFICANT, INDIVIDUALISED discussion involving the patient's VALUES and PRIORITIES regarding future fertility`,
        kp:[
          "Salpingectomy (complete tube removal) is generally preferred when the contralateral tube is healthy - it's definitive (no persistent trophoblast risk from this episode, no future ectopic risk in the removed tube)",
          "Salpingostomy (incision to remove pregnancy, tube preserved) is considered when the contralateral tube is absent/damaged, prioritising preservation - but carries persistent trophoblast risk requiring beta-hCG follow-up similar to methotrexate",
          "A preserved tube after salpingostomy carries an ELEVATED future ectopic risk in that SAME tube, given underlying damage that likely contributed to this episode",
          "Tube removal doesn't mean a patient can't conceive again - the remaining tube (if functional) or IVF remain options",
          "When both tubes are compromised, the discussion becomes highly individualised, weighing tubal preservation attempts against early IVF planning"
        ],
        callouts:[
          {role:"student", type:"exam", text:"A frequently tested concept links salpingostomy DIRECTLY to Module 3's beta-hCG monitoring: 'A patient undergoes salpingostomy. What follow-up is required, and why?' Answer: SERIAL beta-hCG monitoring (similar to methotrexate protocols) is needed because RESIDUAL trophoblastic tissue may remain after salpingostomy ('persistent trophoblast') - if beta-hCG doesn't decline appropriately, FURTHER TREATMENT (methotrexate or repeat surgery) may be needed. This connects SURGICAL and MEDICAL management monitoring concepts."},
          {role:"doctor", type:"clinical", text:"The decision between salpingectomy and salpingostomy should be discussed with the patient BEFORE surgery WHEREVER POSSIBLE (i.e. as part of CONSENT), INCLUDING the scenario where INTRAOPERATIVE FINDINGS might change the PLANNED approach (e.g. a surgeon planning salpingostomy who finds MORE EXTENSIVE damage/bleeding than anticipated may need to CONVERT to salpingectomy) - ensuring CONSENT covers this POSSIBILITY avoids the surgeon facing a decision INTRAOPERATIVELY without the patient's PRIOR input on their preferences."},
          {role:"pharmacist", type:"clinical", text:"Following salpingostomy, if a patient is PRESCRIBED methotrexate as PROPHYLAXIS or TREATMENT for persistent trophoblast (i.e. AFTER surgery, rather than as the PRIMARY treatment discussed in Module 3), the SAME counselling points apply - avoiding folate supplements, NSAIDs, alcohol, and avoiding pregnancy for the specified period. This represents an important CONNECTION between Module 3 (medical management) and Module 4 (surgical management) - methotrexate isn't EXCLUSIVELY a 'PRIMARY' treatment alternative to surgery; it can also be used AFTER surgery for persistent trophoblast."},
          {role:"nurse", type:"clinical", text:"For patients undergoing salpingectomy who express CONCERN about future fertility (a VERY common and valid concern), providing ACCURATE information - that REMOVAL of ONE tube does NOT mean INFERTILITY, and that the REMAINING tube (if healthy) supports FUTURE spontaneous conception, with IVF as an OPTION if needed - delivered with SENSITIVITY to the EMOTIONAL impact of this diagnosis/surgery, is an important part of HOLISTIC care. Connecting patients with appropriate COUNSELLING/SUPPORT resources (Module 6) is also relevant here."},
          {role:"labtech", type:"practical", text:"For patients undergoing salpingostomy, POST-OPERATIVE beta-hCG monitoring (similar timing/frequency considerations to methotrexate protocols - Module 3) is essential to detect PERSISTENT TROPHOBLAST. Ensuring these samples are PROCESSED and RESULTS COMMUNICATED according to the SAME urgency principles as methotrexate monitoring (since INADEQUATE DECLINE has SIMILAR clinical implications - need for further treatment) supports CONTINUITY of the monitoring pathway REGARDLESS of whether the INITIAL management was medical or surgical."},
        ]
      },
      {
        h:"🏥 Surgical Approach — Laparoscopy vs Laparotomy & Perioperative Care",
        a:`If salpingectomy/salpingostomy describes WHAT is done to the tube, the surgical APPROACH - laparoscopic ('keyhole') versus laparotomy (open) - describes HOW the surgeon accesses the abdomen to DO it. The choice between these approaches is heavily influenced by the patient's HAEMODYNAMIC STATUS - a direct, practical link to the spectrum of presentation discussed in Module 2.`,
        c:`LAPAROSCOPIC SURGERY:\n→ The PREFERRED approach for HAEMODYNAMICALLY STABLE patients - involves SMALL incisions, a camera (laparoscope), and specialised instruments to perform salpingectomy or salpingostomy\n→ ADVANTAGES: SHORTER hospital stay, FASTER recovery, LESS postoperative pain, SMALLER scars, generally LOWER rates of certain complications (e.g. adhesion formation) compared to open surgery\n→ REQUIRES: appropriate EQUIPMENT and SURGICAL EXPERTISE - availability may vary by SETTING, which is a PRACTICAL consideration in RESOURCE-VARIED environments\n\nLAPAROTOMY (OPEN SURGERY):\n→ Involves a LARGER abdominal incision, providing DIRECT access and visualisation\n→ GENERALLY INDICATED when:\n  → The patient is HAEMODYNAMICALLY UNSTABLE (Module 6) - RAPID access and the ability to QUICKLY CONTROL BLEEDING is PRIORITISED over the benefits of a minimally invasive approach in this EMERGENCY context\n  → SIGNIFICANT HAEMOPERITONEUM (large amount of blood in the abdomen) is present/anticipated, making laparoscopic visualisation DIFFICULT\n  → LAPAROSCOPIC equipment/expertise is NOT AVAILABLE (a PRACTICAL/RESOURCE consideration)\n  → COMPLEX cases (e.g. extensive adhesions from previous surgeries) where laparoscopic approach may not be FEASIBLE or SAFE\n→ In an UNSTABLE patient, the PRIORITY is RAPID HAEMORRHAGE CONTROL - the 'best' surgical approach in this context is the one that can be performed MOST QUICKLY and EFFECTIVELY by the AVAILABLE team, which is OFTEN laparotomy in a TRULY UNSTABLE patient, though some highly experienced laparoscopic surgeons MAY proceed laparoscopically even in SOME unstable cases if they can do so SAFELY and RAPIDLY - this is a NUANCED, EXPERIENCE-DEPENDENT decision\n\nPERIOPERATIVE CONSIDERATIONS:\n→ ANAESTHESIA: GENERAL ANAESTHESIA is typically required for surgical management of ectopic pregnancy - relevant Autonomic Pharmacology and General Pharmacology concepts (e.g. effects of anaesthetic agents on autonomic tone, drug interactions) apply as for ANY surgical patient, with ADDITIONAL consideration of the patient's CURRENT HAEMODYNAMIC STATUS informing anaesthetic management (e.g. a HYPOVOLAEMIC patient requires DIFFERENT anaesthetic considerations than a STABLE one - relevant to RAPID SEQUENCE INDUCTION considerations in UNSTABLE patients)\n→ ANTI-D PROPHYLAXIS: for Rh-NEGATIVE patients undergoing SURGICAL management (or, depending on guidelines, in some cases of CONFIRMED ectopic regardless of management type), ANTI-D IMMUNOGLOBULIN should be administered to prevent RHESUS SENSITISATION - this is COVERED FURTHER in Module 6, but is a RELEVANT PERIOPERATIVE/PERI-TREATMENT consideration REGARDLESS of WHICH management approach (medical, surgical, or expectant - Module 5) is used\n→ VENOUS THROMBOEMBOLISM (VTE) PROPHYLAXIS: as for OTHER surgical/pregnancy-related admissions, VTE RISK ASSESSMENT and APPROPRIATE PROPHYLAXIS (mechanical and/or pharmacological, depending on individual risk assessment) should be considered as part of PERIOPERATIVE care\n\nPOSTOPERATIVE CARE & DISCHARGE CONSIDERATIONS:\n→ For SALPINGOSTOMY patients: as discussed, beta-hCG FOLLOW-UP is REQUIRED postoperatively\n→ For ALL patients: PAIN MANAGEMENT, monitoring for POSTOPERATIVE COMPLICATIONS (e.g. infection, bleeding), and - importantly - PSYCHOLOGICAL SUPPORT and INFORMATION regarding the DIAGNOSIS, its implications, and FUTURE PREGNANCY PLANNING (Module 6) are all important components of COMPREHENSIVE care, REGARDLESS of the specific surgical approach used`,
        kp:[
          "Laparoscopy is preferred for haemodynamically STABLE patients - faster recovery, less pain, smaller scars; requires appropriate equipment/expertise",
          "Laparotomy is generally indicated for haemodynamically UNSTABLE patients (rapid haemorrhage control prioritised), significant haemoperitoneum, or when laparoscopic resources/expertise aren't available",
          "Anaesthetic management must account for the patient's CURRENT haemodynamic status - an unstable/hypovolaemic patient requires different considerations than a stable one",
          "Anti-D prophylaxis for Rh-negative patients is relevant REGARDLESS of management approach (medical, surgical, or expectant) - covered further in Module 6",
          "Postoperative care includes beta-hCG follow-up (if salpingostomy), pain management, complication monitoring, AND psychological support/future pregnancy planning - regardless of surgical approach"
        ],
        callouts:[
          {role:"student", type:"exam", text:"A commonly tested principle links haemodynamic status DIRECTLY to surgical approach choice: 'A patient with a ruptured ectopic and signs of hypovolaemic shock requires which surgical approach, and why?' Answer: LAPAROTOMY is generally indicated in this scenario - the PRIORITY is RAPID access for HAEMORRHAGE CONTROL, which open surgery typically allows MORE QUICKLY than laparoscopy in a SIGNIFICANTLY UNSTABLE patient, even though laparoscopy would otherwise be PREFERRED for its recovery benefits in a STABLE patient. This is a DIRECT application of Module 2's spectrum-of-presentation concept to Module 4's management choices."},
          {role:"doctor", type:"clinical", text:"In an UNSTABLE patient requiring URGENT surgery, ANAESTHETIC and SURGICAL teams must work in CLOSE COORDINATION - SIMULTANEOUS RESUSCITATION (IV access, fluids, blood products as needed - Module 6) and PREPARATION for SURGERY should occur IN PARALLEL, not sequentially, to MINIMISE the time to HAEMORRHAGE CONTROL. This represents a key MULTIDISCIPLINARY TEAM principle that will be expanded in Module 6."},
          {role:"pharmacist", type:"clinical", text:"For ANY patient undergoing surgical management of ectopic pregnancy, ENSURE Rh STATUS is checked and, if Rh-NEGATIVE, that ANTI-D IMMUNOGLOBULIN is APPROPRIATELY PRESCRIBED and ADMINISTERED according to local protocols - this is a SIMPLE but ESSENTIAL check that, if MISSED, could have significant implications for the patient's FUTURE pregnancies (Rhesus sensitisation - Module 6 covers this in more detail)."},
          {role:"nurse", type:"clinical", text:"Postoperatively, REGARDLESS of WHETHER salpingectomy or salpingostomy was performed, providing the patient with CLEAR information about WHAT WAS DONE (many patients, having consented PREOPERATIVELY to 'POSSIBLE' salpingectomy OR salpingostomy depending on findings, may not be CERTAIN which was ACTUALLY performed once they wake from anaesthesia) - and what FOLLOW-UP (if any, e.g. beta-hCG monitoring after salpingostomy) is needed, is an important part of DISCHARGE PLANNING and patient understanding."},
          {role:"labtech", type:"practical", text:"PERIOPERATIVELY, GROUP AND SAVE (or CROSSMATCH, depending on anticipated blood loss/availability) should be available for ANY patient undergoing surgery for ectopic pregnancy, GIVEN the POTENTIAL for SIGNIFICANT INTRAOPERATIVE BLEEDING even in PLANNED laparoscopic cases (conversion to open surgery due to UNEXPECTED bleeding can occur) - this PREPAREDNESS, even for CASES PLANNED as 'routine' laparoscopic procedures, reflects appropriate ANTICIPATION of POTENTIAL escalation."},
        ]
      },
    ],
    ev:"RCOG Green-top Guideline on Ectopic Pregnancy and Miscarriage; NICE Guideline NG126; ESHRE Guideline on Diagnosis and Management of Ectopic Pregnancy; Cochrane Review on Surgical Treatment for Tubal Ectopic Pregnancy"
  },
  {
    id:5, num:"05", icon:"🔍", free:false, dur:"2h 30m", lessons:6, color:"#B7950B",
    title:"Expectant Management & Special Situations", sub:"Watchful Waiting, Interstitial/Cervical/Caesarean Scar Ectopics & Heterotopic Pregnancy",
    aud:["doctor","nurse","pharmacist","labtech","student"],
    tagline:"Not every ectopic pregnancy requires immediate treatment - but the 'special situations' covered here are exactly where assumptions from the 'typical' tubal ectopic can become dangerous.",
    story:`Early Pregnancy Assessment Unit, follow-up clinic. Following on from Module 3's protocols, a patient - Abena Owusu, 26 - has a very LOW beta-hCG of 180 mIU/mL, which has been FALLING on repeat testing (from 250 two days earlier), with NO visible intrauterine OR ectopic pregnancy on ultrasound, and she remains COMPLETELY ASYMPTOMATIC.\n\n"In this situation," explains Dr. Adjei-Mensah, "with such a LOW and FALLING beta-hCG, and you feeling COMPLETELY WELL, we may be able to simply WATCH and WAIT - what's called EXPECTANT MANAGEMENT. Many pregnancies at THIS stage, with THESE numbers, will RESOLVE ON THEIR OWN without ANY treatment - whether this represents a VERY early miscarriage or a small, resolving ectopic, the body may handle it WITHOUT intervention."\n\n"That sounds easier," Abena says. "But how do you know it's SAFE to just wait?"\n\n"We DON'T know with ABSOLUTE certainty for any INDIVIDUAL," Dr. Adjei-Mensah acknowledges honestly. "But your NUMBERS and SYMPTOMS put you in a CATEGORY where expectant management has been shown to be a REASONABLE, SAFE OPTION for MOST patients - PROVIDED we CONTINUE monitoring your beta-hCG until it's UNDETECTABLE, and PROVIDED you understand the SAME safety-netting we'd give for ANY ectopic monitoring - any worsening pain, bleeding, dizziness, or shoulder pain means coming back IMMEDIATELY, regardless of how 'low risk' your numbers currently look."\n\nLater that same clinic session, a DIFFERENT patient - Efua Asante, 33, with a PREVIOUS CAESAREAN SECTION - is found to have a pregnancy implanted DIRECTLY within her CAESAREAN SCAR.\n\n"This is a DIFFERENT situation entirely," Dr. Adjei-Mensah explains to the team afterward. "A caesarean SCAR ectopic carries its OWN SPECIFIC risks - this is NOT something we'd manage with SIMPLE expectant management, even with similar numbers to Abena's case, because of WHERE this is and the tissue involved."\n\nThis module covers BOTH ends of this spectrum - when 'watching and waiting' is appropriate, and the SPECIAL, HIGHER-RISK situations where the USUAL approaches need SIGNIFICANT modification.`,
    sections:[
      {
        h:"⏳ Expectant Management — Criteria, Process & Limitations",
        a:`If methotrexate is 'helping the process along' and surgery is 'directly intervening,' expectant management is essentially 'letting the body's own processes take their course' - but this is NOT the same as 'doing nothing.' It's an ACTIVE MANAGEMENT DECISION with its OWN SPECIFIC CRITERIA, MONITORING REQUIREMENTS, and LIMITATIONS - appropriate for a SPECIFIC, CAREFULLY-SELECTED subset of patients.`,
        c:`EXPECTANT MANAGEMENT — WHAT IT IS:\n→ A management approach involving CLOSE MONITORING (serial beta-hCG, +/- ultrasound) WITHOUT immediate medical (methotrexate) or surgical intervention, based on the EXPECTATION that the pregnancy will RESOLVE SPONTANEOUSLY (the trophoblastic tissue will naturally regress and beta-hCG will fall to undetectable levels)\n→ This is DISTINCT from 'doing nothing' - it involves STRUCTURED FOLLOW-UP and CLEAR CRITERIA for when to CHANGE to ACTIVE management (medical or surgical) if the expected resolution does NOT occur, or if SYMPTOMS develop\n\nGENERAL CRITERIA FOR CONSIDERING EXPECTANT MANAGEMENT:\n→ Patient is ASYMPTOMATIC or has only MINIMAL symptoms, and is HAEMODYNAMICALLY STABLE\n→ beta-hCG is LOW (specific thresholds vary by guideline, but GENERALLY considerably LOWER than methotrexate eligibility thresholds) AND DECLINING on serial measurement (a beta-hCG that is STATIC or RISING is LESS suitable for expectant management)\n→ NO significant free fluid/haemoperitoneum on ultrasound\n→ Often used in the context of 'PREGNANCY OF UNKNOWN LOCATION' (Module 2) with these REASSURING features, where the EXACT location/nature (very early failing intrauterine pregnancy vs small resolving ectopic) may NEVER be definitively determined - and for MANY patients meeting these criteria, this DISTINCTION may NOT ultimately matter CLINICALLY, as BOTH would be expected to resolve similarly\n→ Patient UNDERSTANDS and ACCEPTS the approach, INCLUDING the COMMITMENT to ONGOING MONITORING and CLEAR safety-netting\n\nTHE MONITORING PROCESS:\n→ SERIAL beta-hCG measurements (frequency per local protocol - often every 48 HOURS initially, similar to PUL assessment) UNTIL levels reach UNDETECTABLE/NON-PREGNANT range\n→ If beta-hCG FAILS to decline appropriately, PLATEAUS, or RISES at ANY point, REASSESSMENT for ACTIVE management (methotrexate or surgery, depending on the FULL clinical picture at THAT point) is indicated\n→ If the patient develops ANY symptoms (pain, bleeding changes, dizziness, shoulder pain) at ANY point during monitoring, URGENT REASSESSMENT is required, REGARDLESS of the beta-hCG trend\n\nLIMITATIONS & CONSIDERATIONS:\n→ Expectant management requires PATIENT RELIABILITY for FOLLOW-UP - patients who may have DIFFICULTY ATTENDING repeated appointments (e.g. due to DISTANCE, work, or other access barriers - a PARTICULARLY RELEVANT consideration in SOME settings) may be LESS suitable candidates, as the SAFETY of this approach DEPENDS on this monitoring actually occurring\n→ Even with REASSURING initial numbers, a SMALL PROPORTION of patients managed expectantly will ULTIMATELY require ACTIVE intervention - this should be DISCUSSED as part of the DECISION, not presented as a 'guaranteed no-treatment' pathway\n→ The PSYCHOLOGICAL ASPECT of 'waiting' for an UNCERTAIN outcome (without the sense of 'doing something' that medical/surgical treatment might provide) can be DIFFICULT for SOME patients - this is a LEGITIMATE consideration in SHARED DECISION-MAKING, not merely a 'preference' issue`,
        kp:[
          "Expectant management = structured monitoring (serial beta-hCG until undetectable) expecting spontaneous resolution - it's an ACTIVE management decision with clear criteria for escalation, not 'doing nothing'",
          "Criteria: asymptomatic/minimal symptoms, haemodynamically stable, LOW and DECLINING beta-hCG, no significant free fluid - often used in 'pregnancy of unknown location' with reassuring features",
          "If beta-hCG fails to decline/plateaus/rises, OR symptoms develop at any point, reassessment for active management is needed regardless of where in the monitoring process",
          "Patient reliability for follow-up is essential - access barriers to repeated appointments may make a patient less suitable, since safety depends on monitoring actually occurring",
          "A small proportion of expectantly-managed patients will ultimately need active intervention - this should be part of informed discussion, and the psychological burden of 'waiting' is a legitimate consideration"
        ],
        callouts:[
          {role:"student", type:"exam", text:"A commonly tested distinction: 'How does expectant management differ from simply discharging a patient with no follow-up?' Answer: expectant management involves STRUCTURED, ONGOING MONITORING (serial beta-hCG) with CLEAR CRITERIA for when to SWITCH to active management - it is an ACTIVE clinical pathway, not an ABSENCE of management. A question describing a patient discharged with 'no follow-up arranged' after a borderline beta-hCG result would represent INADEQUATE care, NOT appropriate expectant management."},
          {role:"doctor", type:"clinical", text:"When DISCUSSING expectant management with a patient, FRAME it ACCURATELY: this is a RECOGNISED, EVIDENCE-BASED approach for CAREFULLY SELECTED patients - NOT a 'lesser' option chosen due to resource constraints (though in SOME settings, resource considerations may ALSO factor into overall care pathways). Ensure the patient understands BOTH the LIKELY outcome (spontaneous resolution) AND the POSSIBILITY of needing FURTHER treatment, framed HONESTLY without causing UNDUE alarm."},
          {role:"pharmacist", type:"clinical", text:"Patients undergoing expectant management may experience SIMILAR ANXIETY to those undergoing methotrexate monitoring (Module 3) during the WAITING period - similar SUPPORTIVE counselling (explaining WHY monitoring is necessary, reinforcing safety-netting) applies. Additionally, ensure patients understand that EXPECTANT management does NOT mean 'no precautions needed' - the SAME advice regarding AVOIDING PREGNANCY until resolution is confirmed, and SEEKING care for RED FLAG symptoms, applies."},
          {role:"nurse", type:"clinical", text:"For patients undergoing expectant management, PRACTICAL SUPPORT around APPOINTMENT SCHEDULING - ensuring patients have CLEAR, WRITTEN information about WHEN to return for repeat bloods, and ASSESSING (sensitively) for ANY BARRIERS to attending (transport, work commitments, costs) - can directly impact the SAFETY of this approach, since the approach's safety RELIES on monitoring actually happening. Where barriers are identified, discussing ALTERNATIVES (e.g. closer/more accessible testing locations if available) may be relevant."},
          {role:"labtech", type:"practical", text:"For patients on expectant management, serial beta-hCG samples carry the SAME INTERPRETIVE IMPORTANCE as PUL/methotrexate monitoring (Modules 2-3) - a result showing an UNEXPECTED RISE or PLATEAU (rather than the EXPECTED decline) should be FLAGGED for PROMPT clinical attention, as this would change the management pathway from 'continue watching' to 'active reassessment needed.'"},
        ]
      },
      {
        h:"⚠️ Special Situations — Interstitial, Cervical, Caesarean Scar Ectopics & Heterotopic Pregnancy",
        a:`The 'typical' tubal ectopic pregnancy - and the management principles covered in Modules 3-4 - apply to the VAST MAJORITY of cases. But a SMALL, IMPORTANT subset of ectopic pregnancies occur in locations where the USUAL 'rules' need SIGNIFICANT MODIFICATION - either because of PROXIMITY to MAJOR BLOOD VESSELS (making rupture potentially CATASTROPHIC), or because of the SPECIFIC TISSUE involved (e.g. scar tissue, which behaves DIFFERENTLY from normal tubal tissue).`,
        c:`INTERSTITIAL ECTOPIC PREGNANCY (revisited from Module 1):\n→ Located within the MUSCULAR WALL of the uterus, at the point where the fallopian tube ENTERS the uterus - this region has a RICH BLOOD SUPPLY (close to the UTERINE and OVARIAN vessels)\n→ KEY RISK: because of the SURROUNDING MUSCULAR TISSUE (compared to the THIN-WALLED tube), an interstitial ectopic may GROW LARGER before becoming SYMPTOMATIC or being DETECTED - and IF RUPTURE occurs, the PROXIMITY to MAJOR VESSELS means BLEEDING CAN BE RAPID and SEVERE, potentially MORE SO than a 'typical' tubal rupture\n→ MANAGEMENT: may involve METHOTREXATE (medical management may be considered, sometimes with MODIFIED protocols/closer monitoring given the location) OR SURGERY - surgical management of interstitial ectopics is TECHNICALLY MORE CHALLENGING than a typical tubal ectopic, given the location and vascularity, and may require SPECIALIST input\n\nCERVICAL ECTOPIC PREGNANCY:\n→ Implantation WITHIN the CERVICAL CANAL - RARE, but the CERVIX has a RICH VASCULAR SUPPLY, and pregnancy tissue implanted here can be ASSOCIATED WITH SIGNIFICANT HAEMORRHAGE if DISTURBED (e.g. during ATTEMPTED removal via curettage, which may be INITIALLY (and INAPPROPRIATELY) attempted if the ectopic LOCATION isn't recognised, mistaking it for a CERVICAL miscarriage)\n→ MANAGEMENT: METHOTREXATE may be used (sometimes with ADDITIONAL techniques, e.g. UTERINE ARTERY EMBOLISATION in some cases, to reduce vascularity before/alongside methotrexate) - SURGICAL approaches carry SIGNIFICANT HAEMORRHAGE RISK and may, in SEVERE cases, require procedures up to and including HYSTERECTOMY if BLEEDING cannot otherwise be controlled - though this represents an EXTREME outcome, not a 'typical' management step\n\nCAESAREAN SCAR ECTOPIC PREGNANCY:\n→ Implantation WITHIN the scar tissue of a PREVIOUS CAESAREAN SECTION - INCREASINGLY RECOGNISED as a DISTINCT entity as CAESAREAN RATES have risen GLOBALLY\n→ KEY RISK: the SCAR TISSUE is STRUCTURALLY DIFFERENT from normal myometrium - it may be THINNER and have ALTERED VASCULARITY - a pregnancy implanting HERE carries risk of UTERINE RUPTURE (if allowed to progress) and SIGNIFICANT HAEMORRHAGE\n→ DISTINGUISHING FROM A 'NORMAL' LOW-LYING INTRAUTERINE PREGNANCY: this is a CRITICAL diagnostic point - a caesarean scar ectopic is implanted WITHIN the SCAR DEFECT itself, NOT simply 'low in the uterus' - careful ULTRASOUND assessment (sometimes requiring SPECIALIST imaging expertise) is needed to make this distinction, as MANAGEMENT differs SIGNIFICANTLY\n→ MANAGEMENT: options include METHOTREXATE (sometimes LOCAL injection directly into the gestational sac, in addition to or instead of SYSTEMIC administration, depending on protocols), SURGICAL approaches (which may include HYSTEROSCOPIC or LAPAROSCOPIC techniques depending on the case), or COMBINATIONS - this is an area where MANAGEMENT should be GUIDED BY SPECIALIST teams given the COMPLEXITY and EVOLVING evidence base\n\nHETEROTOPIC PREGNANCY:\n→ SIMULTANEOUS coexistence of an INTRAUTERINE pregnancy AND an ECTOPIC pregnancy\n→ RARE in SPONTANEOUS conception (historically estimated at a VERY low rate), but SIGNIFICANTLY MORE COMMON with ASSISTED REPRODUCTION (particularly where MULTIPLE embryos are transferred)\n→ CRITICAL DIAGNOSTIC CHALLENGE: the PRESENCE of a VISIBLE INTRAUTERINE PREGNANCY on ultrasound can provide FALSE REASSURANCE, potentially leading to an ECTOPIC pregnancy being MISSED/OVERLOOKED, particularly if the CLINICIAN's attention is 'satisfied' by seeing the intrauterine pregnancy and doesn't CONTINUE looking for an ADDITIONAL ectopic\n→ MANAGEMENT IMPLICATIONS: METHOTREXATE is CONTRAINDICATED if the INTRAUTERINE pregnancy is DESIRED/viable (as discussed in Module 3) - SURGICAL management of the ECTOPIC component (typically laparoscopic salpingectomy, if tubal) is generally the approach, performed with CARE to AVOID disrupting the INTRAUTERINE pregnancy\n→ RISK FACTOR AWARENESS: in patients who have undergone ASSISTED REPRODUCTION WITH MULTIPLE EMBRYO TRANSFER, even AFTER an intrauterine pregnancy is CONFIRMED, MAINTAINING AWARENESS of heterotopic pregnancy POSSIBILITY (e.g. if the patient develops PAIN or other symptoms DESPITE a confirmed intrauterine pregnancy) is IMPORTANT - this should NOT be DISMISSED simply because 'the pregnancy has already been confirmed as intrauterine'`,
        kp:[
          "Interstitial ectopics: rich blood supply near the uterus means rupture can cause RAPID, SEVERE bleeding - may grow larger before detection; management may be medical or surgical, often requiring specialist input",
          "Cervical ectopics: rich cervical vascular supply means significant haemorrhage risk, especially if disturbed (e.g. mistaken attempted curettage); management may include methotrexate +/- embolisation, with surgery carrying significant haemorrhage risk",
          "Caesarean scar ectopics: implanted WITHIN the scar defect (not just 'low-lying') - structurally altered tissue carries uterine rupture/haemorrhage risk if progression occurs; requires specialist-guided, individualised management",
          "Heterotopic pregnancy (intrauterine + ectopic together) is rare spontaneously but more common with multiple-embryo ART transfer - a visible intrauterine pregnancy can provide FALSE REASSURANCE, causing a coexisting ectopic to be missed",
          "In heterotopic pregnancy, methotrexate is contraindicated if the intrauterine pregnancy is desired - surgical management of the ectopic component is the typical approach, performed carefully to protect the intrauterine pregnancy"
        ],
        callouts:[
          {role:"student", type:"exam", text:"Heterotopic pregnancy is a HIGH-YIELD 'don't stop looking' concept: 'A patient who conceived via IVF with TWO embryos transferred has a CONFIRMED intrauterine pregnancy on ultrasound, but presents with persistent unilateral pain. What should be considered?' Answer: HETEROTOPIC PREGNANCY should remain on the differential DESPITE the confirmed intrauterine pregnancy - the VISIBLE intrauterine pregnancy does NOT EXCLUDE a coexisting ectopic, particularly in a patient with ELEVATED heterotopic risk (multiple embryo transfer). This tests whether you understand that ONE finding (confirmed IUP) doesn't ALWAYS 'complete' the diagnostic picture."},
          {role:"doctor", type:"clinical", text:"For CAESAREAN SCAR ectopic pregnancy, the DISTINCTION from a 'low-lying intrauterine pregnancy' is CRUCIAL because the MANAGEMENT and RISK PROFILE differ SIGNIFICANTLY - a low-lying but TRULY intrauterine pregnancy may simply be MONITORED (with awareness of placental implications later in pregnancy if it continues), whereas a TRUE caesarean scar ECTOPIC carries SIGNIFICANT EARLY risks requiring ACTIVE management discussion. When UNCERTAIN, SPECIALIST IMAGING REVIEW is appropriate rather than PROCEEDING based on an AMBIGUOUS initial assessment."},
          {role:"pharmacist", type:"clinical", text:"If LOCAL (intra-gestational sac) methotrexate injection is used for a caesarean scar ectopic (an approach used in SOME specialist settings), this represents a DIFFERENT ADMINISTRATION ROUTE from the SYSTEMIC (intramuscular) administration covered in Module 3 - while the GENERAL counselling points (avoiding folate supplements, NSAIDs, alcohol, pregnancy avoidance) remain RELEVANT, the SPECIFIC PROCEDURE and MONITORING for LOCAL administration may differ, and pharmacists should be aware that 'methotrexate for ectopic pregnancy' is NOT a single, uniform protocol across ALL ectopic locations."},
          {role:"nurse", type:"clinical", text:"For a patient with a PREVIOUS CAESAREAN SECTION presenting with a NEW pregnancy, NOTING this history PROMINENTLY and ENSURING it's COMMUNICATED to the team performing ultrasound assessment helps PROMPT specific consideration of caesarean scar ectopic as part of the assessment - this history might OTHERWISE be 'buried' in a general obstetric history without being FLAGGED as DIRECTLY RELEVANT to the CURRENT presentation's differential diagnosis."},
          {role:"labtech", type:"practical", text:"For patients with SPECIAL SITUATION ectopics (interstitial, cervical, caesarean scar) undergoing methotrexate or other monitored treatments, the SAME beta-hCG monitoring PRINCIPLES apply as Module 3 - but given the POTENTIALLY HIGHER SEVERITY of complications if rupture/haemorrhage occurs at THESE locations, ENSURING beta-hCG (and FBC, if bleeding is a concern) results are PROCESSED and COMMUNICATED WITHOUT DELAY may be EVEN MORE CRITICAL than for a 'typical' tubal ectopic, given the POTENTIALLY MORE RAPID/SEVERE deterioration possible at these sites."},
        ]
      },
    ],
    ev:"RCOG Green-top Guideline on Ectopic Pregnancy and Miscarriage; NICE Guideline NG126; ESHRE Guideline on Diagnosis and Management of Ectopic Pregnancy; Society for Maternal-Fetal Medicine Consult Series on Caesarean Scar Pregnancy"
  },
  {
    id:6, num:"06", icon:"🚨", free:false, dur:"2h 30m", lessons:6, color:"#641E16",
    title:"Emergency Recognition & Multidisciplinary Care", sub:"Ruptured Ectopic, Resuscitation, Transfusion, Anti-D & Holistic Follow-Up",
    aud:["doctor","nurse","pharmacist","labtech","student"],
    tagline:"When everything covered in this course converges into a single, time-critical emergency - and what comes after, for the person whose pregnancy this was.",
    story:`Resuscitation Bay, Emergency Department, Korle-Bu Teaching Hospital. Paramedics bring in Akua Darko, 30, who collapsed at home. She is PALE, CLAMMY, with a heart rate of 130, blood pressure 78/45, and is barely responsive. Her partner reports she has been having "some lower belly pain for a couple of days" and missed her last period.\n\n"This is a RUPTURED ECTOPIC until proven otherwise," states Dr. Owusu, the emergency consultant, immediately initiating the team's MASSIVE HAEMORRHAGE protocol. "PREGNANCY TEST NOW. TWO LARGE-BORE IV LINES. CROSSMATCH FOUR UNITS. CALL GYNAECOLOGY AND THEATRE - we need them HERE, NOW, not after results come back."\n\nWithin minutes: the pregnancy test is POSITIVE. A bedside ultrasound (FAST scan) shows FREE FLUID in the abdomen. The gynaecology team arrives, confirms the clinical picture, and Akua is taken DIRECTLY to theatre for an EMERGENCY LAPAROTOMY - there is NO TIME for the 'usual' sequence of beta-hCG trends, discriminatory zones, or scheduled ultrasounds discussed in earlier modules. In theatre, a ruptured tubal ectopic with significant haemoperitoneum is found and managed with salpingectomy; Akua receives TRANSFUSED BLOOD during the procedure and stabilises.\n\nThree days later, before discharge, the team checks Akua's blood group - she is RH-NEGATIVE. ANTI-D IMMUNOGLOBULIN is administered.\n\nBefore she leaves, a midwife sits with Akua. "I know everything has happened SO fast," she says gently. "Before you go, I want to talk about what this means going forward - for your body's recovery, for FUTURE pregnancies if and when you're ready, and also just... how you're FEELING about everything that's happened. This has been a significant loss, on top of a frightening medical emergency, and BOTH of those things deserve attention."\n\nThis final module brings together the EMERGENCY recognition and resuscitation principles for the WORST-CASE scenario this course has been building toward - and the HOLISTIC, MULTIDISCIPLINARY care that EVERY patient with ectopic pregnancy deserves, regardless of HOW their case unfolded.`,
    sections:[
      {
        h:"🆘 Recognising & Resuscitating the Ruptured Ectopic",
        a:`Everything covered in Modules 1-5 - risk factors, presentations, diagnostic pathways, management options - exists on a SPECTRUM. This section is about the FAR END of that spectrum: when an ectopic pregnancy RUPTURES, causing SIGNIFICANT INTERNAL HAEMORRHAGE, and the situation transforms INSTANTLY from a 'diagnostic puzzle' into a TIME-CRITICAL RESUSCITATION.`,
        c:`RECOGNISING THE RUPTURED/HAEMORRHAGING ECTOPIC:\n→ As introduced in Module 2, signs include: SEVERE, often SUDDEN-ONSET abdominal pain; SIGNS OF HYPOVOLAEMIC SHOCK (tachycardia, hypotension, pallor, clamminess, altered consciousness/collapse); PERITONITIC ABDOMEN; SHOULDER TIP PAIN\n→ A KEY PRINCIPLE: in a patient of REPRODUCTIVE AGE presenting with COLLAPSE/SHOCK of UNCLEAR CAUSE, ECTOPIC PREGNANCY MUST BE ACTIVELY CONSIDERED AND EXCLUDED (via PREGNANCY TEST) as PART OF INITIAL ASSESSMENT - this should occur IN PARALLEL with, NOT AFTER, initial resuscitation measures\n\nIMMEDIATE MANAGEMENT - THE 'ABC' APPROACH WITH OBSTETRIC AWARENESS:\n→ AIRWAY/BREATHING: standard assessment and support as needed\n→ CIRCULATION: \n  → ESTABLISH LARGE-BORE IV ACCESS (typically TWO large-bore cannulae)\n  → SEND BLOODS IMMEDIATELY: FULL BLOOD COUNT, GROUP AND CROSSMATCH (anticipating TRANSFUSION need), COAGULATION studies, and a PREGNANCY TEST if not already confirmed\n  → COMMENCE FLUID RESUSCITATION (typically crystalloid initially) WHILE AWAITING BLOOD PRODUCTS\n  → ACTIVATE MASSIVE HAEMORRHAGE PROTOCOL if appropriate per local guidelines, based on the SEVERITY of presentation - this typically involves RAPID ACCESS to BLOOD PRODUCTS (red cells, and depending on protocol/severity, plasma/platelets) WITHOUT WAITING for FULL crossmatch results in the MOST URGENT situations (using O-NEGATIVE or GROUP-SPECIFIC blood as an INTERIM measure per local protocol)\n→ SIMULTANEOUS ESCALATION: URGENT involvement of GYNAECOLOGY/OBSTETRICS and ANAESTHETICS/THEATRE teams should occur IMMEDIATELY, IN PARALLEL with resuscitation - NOT sequentially after 'completing' initial assessment\n→ BEDSIDE IMAGING: a FOCUSED ASSESSMENT WITH SONOGRAPHY (FAST-type scan, or dedicated obstetric/gynaecological bedside ultrasound if available) can RAPIDLY identify FREE FLUID (suggesting haemoperitoneum) and SUPPORT the clinical diagnosis WITHOUT DELAYING transfer to definitive care (theatre)\n\nDEFINITIVE MANAGEMENT:\n→ EMERGENCY SURGERY (typically LAPAROTOMY given INSTABILITY, as discussed in Module 4) to ACHIEVE HAEMORRHAGE CONTROL - this is the DEFINITIVE step, and ALL preceding actions (resuscitation, bloods, imaging) should be FOCUSED ON GETTING THE PATIENT TO THIS POINT AS RAPIDLY AND SAFELY AS POSSIBLE\n→ INTRAOPERATIVE: ongoing RESUSCITATION continues DURING surgery (anaesthetic team manages this in COORDINATION with the surgical team) - TRANSFUSION of blood products occurs AS NEEDED based on ONGOING blood loss and patient response\n\nTHE 'TIME IS TISSUE/TIME IS BLOOD' PRINCIPLE:\nIn a RUPTURED ectopic with ONGOING bleeding, DELAY at ANY STAGE - diagnostic, resuscitative, or surgical - directly translates to MORE BLOOD LOSS and WORSE OUTCOMES. This is WHY the 'usual' sequential diagnostic pathway (Module 2's beta-hCG trends, scheduled ultrasounds) is ABANDONED in this scenario - the CLINICAL PICTURE of shock in a patient with a positive pregnancy test IS SUFFICIENT to PROCEED DIRECTLY toward EMERGENCY SURGERY, with CONFIRMATORY information (bedside ultrasound, bloods) GATHERED IN PARALLEL rather than SEQUENTIALLY`,
        kp:[
          "In a reproductive-age patient with collapse/shock of unclear cause, ectopic pregnancy must be actively considered (via pregnancy test) as part of INITIAL assessment, in PARALLEL with resuscitation - not after",
          "Immediate management: large-bore IV access, urgent bloods (FBC, group/crossmatch, coagulation, pregnancy test), fluid resuscitation, and massive haemorrhage protocol activation if appropriate",
          "Gynaecology/obstetrics and anaesthetics/theatre teams should be escalated IMMEDIATELY and in PARALLEL with resuscitation, not sequentially",
          "Bedside ultrasound (FAST-type) can rapidly identify free fluid without delaying transfer to definitive care",
          "'Time is blood': in a ruptured ectopic with ongoing bleeding, the usual sequential diagnostic pathway is ABANDONED - shock + positive pregnancy test is sufficient to proceed toward emergency surgery, with confirmatory information gathered in PARALLEL"
        ],
        callouts:[
          {role:"student", type:"exam", text:"A frequently tested 'systems' question: 'A 28-year-old collapses with hypotension and tachycardia of unclear cause. List your IMMEDIATE priority actions.' A STRONG answer INCLUDES pregnancy test as part of INITIAL bloods/assessment ALONGSIDE standard ABC resuscitation steps - candidates who OMIT this, or who list it as a 'LATER' step AFTER initial stabilisation, may be missing a TIME-CRITICAL diagnosis. This module's CORE message - that ectopic pregnancy assessment happens IN PARALLEL with, not AFTER, resuscitation - is the key exam point."},
          {role:"doctor", type:"clinical", text:"In a TRULY UNSTABLE patient with SUSPECTED ruptured ectopic, do NOT WAIT for a FORMAL, COMPREHENSIVE ultrasound report or COMPLETE blood results BEFORE ESCALATING to gynaecology/theatre - PARALLEL PROCESSING (bloods sent + imaging requested + gynaecology called + resuscitation commenced, ALL SIMULTANEOUSLY) is the goal. A patient who is CLINICALLY DETERIORATING should PROCEED toward THEATRE based on the CLINICAL PICTURE, with CONFIRMATORY information arriving ALONGSIDE rather than DELAYING this process."},
          {role:"pharmacist", type:"clinical", text:"In a MASSIVE HAEMORRHAGE scenario, pharmacy services may be involved in ENSURING RAPID AVAILABILITY of BLOOD PRODUCTS and, where relevant, OTHER medications used in MASSIVE TRANSFUSION protocols (e.g. TRANEXAMIC ACID, which has a role in REDUCING MORTALITY from significant haemorrhage in various contexts and may be considered as part of MASSIVE HAEMORRHAGE management per local protocols) - FAMILIARITY with the LOCAL MASSIVE HAEMORRHAGE PROTOCOL and the PHARMACY's role within it supports RAPID RESPONSE in these TIME-CRITICAL situations."},
          {role:"nurse", type:"clinical", text:"In a RESUSCITATION scenario for suspected ruptured ectopic, your role often includes: ESTABLISHING IV ACCESS and SENDING BLOODS RAPIDLY, CONTINUOUS MONITORING of vital signs (recognising TRENDS - e.g. COMPENSATED shock that is BEGINNING to DECOMPENSATE), COMMUNICATING CLEARLY and PROMPTLY with the WIDER TEAM (ensuring gynaecology/theatre have been ALERTED, not assuming 'someone else' has done this), and PREPARING the patient and environment for RAPID TRANSFER to theatre - ALL OF THIS occurring SIMULTANEOUSLY, reflecting the PARALLEL-PROCESSING principle central to this module."},
          {role:"labtech", type:"practical", text:"For a patient presenting with SUSPECTED ruptured ectopic and shock, samples for FULL BLOOD COUNT, GROUP AND CROSSMATCH, and COAGULATION should be treated as the HIGHEST PRIORITY/EMERGENCY samples - results (PARTICULARLY blood group/crossmatch, to enable TRANSFUSION) are NEEDED AS RAPIDLY AS POSSIBLE to support ONGOING resuscitation and SURGERY. In SOME cases, O-NEGATIVE blood may be released BEFORE crossmatch is complete per MASSIVE HAEMORRHAGE protocols - understanding this URGENCY and the POTENTIAL for 'protocol' blood release AHEAD of full crossmatch supports APPROPRIATE PRIORITISATION."},
        ]
      },
      {
        h:"🤝 Anti-D, Follow-Up & Holistic/Psychological Care",
        a:`Once the IMMEDIATE emergency (or, for the MAJORITY of patients, the planned medical/surgical/expectant management) has concluded, the JOURNEY isn't over. This final section covers the IMPORTANT but sometimes OVERLOOKED elements of care that apply REGARDLESS of HOW a patient's ectopic pregnancy was managed - from a SIMPLE injection that protects FUTURE pregnancies, to the EMOTIONAL and PSYCHOLOGICAL dimensions of this diagnosis.`,
        c:`ANTI-D (RHO(D)) IMMUNOGLOBULIN PROPHYLAXIS:\n→ For patients who are RHESUS (Rh) D-NEGATIVE, ANTI-D IMMUNOGLOBULIN should be administered following confirmed ectopic pregnancy, REGARDLESS of the management approach used (medical, surgical, or in SOME guidelines, even expectant management of a CONFIRMED ectopic) - this PREVENTS RHESUS SENSITISATION, which could otherwise cause SIGNIFICANT PROBLEMS (haemolytic disease of the fetus/newborn) in a FUTURE pregnancy if the patient carries an Rh-POSITIVE fetus\n→ This is a SIMPLE, often STRAIGHTFORWARD intervention - but requires that BLOOD GROUP/Rh STATUS is CHECKED and ACTED UPON - a step that can be OVERLOOKED amid the URGENCY of acute management, particularly in EMERGENCY presentations (Section 1) where the FOCUS is, understandably, on IMMEDIATE survival\n→ TIMING/DOSING follows LOCAL PROTOCOLS - the KEY PRINCIPLE for THIS module is AWARENESS that this step is REQUIRED and should be EXPLICITLY CHECKED as part of DISCHARGE PLANNING for EVERY Rh-NEGATIVE patient with confirmed ectopic pregnancy\n\nFOLLOW-UP CARE - SUMMARY ACROSS MANAGEMENT TYPES:\n→ MEDICAL (methotrexate): beta-hCG monitoring until non-pregnant levels (Module 3)\n→ SURGICAL (salpingostomy): beta-hCG monitoring for persistent trophoblast (Module 4); (salpingectomy): generally NO ongoing beta-hCG monitoring needed once the TUBE (and pregnancy) has been REMOVED, though POSTOPERATIVE recovery follow-up still applies\n→ EXPECTANT: beta-hCG monitoring until undetectable (Module 5)\n→ ACROSS ALL TYPES: discussion of WHEN it's SAFE to attempt FUTURE PREGNANCY (varies by management type - e.g. AVOIDING pregnancy for a SPECIFIED period after methotrexate, vs potentially SOONER after surgical management once RECOVERED, though INDIVIDUALISED guidance from the treating team is essential), and discussion of FUTURE ECTOPIC RISK (patients with a PREVIOUS ectopic have an INCREASED risk of a FUTURE ectopic, meaning EARLY review/scan in FUTURE pregnancies is often RECOMMENDED)\n\nPSYCHOLOGICAL & EMOTIONAL CARE:\n→ Ectopic pregnancy represents BOTH a MEDICAL EMERGENCY/CONDITION AND, for MANY patients, a PREGNANCY LOSS - these TWO DIMENSIONS can be EASY to SEPARATE in CLINICAL DOCUMENTATION/discussion, but are EXPERIENCED TOGETHER by the patient\n→ Patients may experience GRIEF (related to the pregnancy loss), ANXIETY (related to the MEDICAL EMERGENCY they may have experienced, and/or CONCERNS about future fertility), and in SOME cases, GUILT or SELF-BLAME (which is NOT warranted - ectopic pregnancy is NOT caused by anything the patient DID or FAILED to do, and this REASSURANCE is important to convey)\n→ For patients who experienced a LIFE-THREATENING EMERGENCY (Section 1), there may ADDITIONALLY be PROCESSING needed around the TRAUMATIC nature of that experience itself - SEPARATE from, though RELATED TO, the pregnancy loss\n→ APPROPRIATE SUPPORT may include: ACKNOWLEDGEMENT of the loss (using LANGUAGE the PATIENT is comfortable with - some patients may NOT think of this as a 'baby loss' in the same way as a later pregnancy loss, while OTHERS may feel this VERY STRONGLY - AVOID ASSUMPTIONS either way, and FOLLOW THE PATIENT'S LEAD), INFORMATION about SUPPORT SERVICES/COUNSELLING where available, and ENSURING the patient knows HOW to seek FURTHER SUPPORT if needed AFTER DISCHARGE (this is NOT something that necessarily 'resolves' by the time of discharge)\n→ PARTNER/FAMILY SUPPORT: where appropriate and with the PATIENT's consent, INVOLVING partners/support people in DISCUSSIONS (particularly following an EMERGENCY presentation, which can be FRIGHTENING for THEM too) is part of HOLISTIC care\n\nFUTURE PREGNANCY PLANNING:\n→ Patients should be INFORMED that, in a FUTURE pregnancy, EARLY REVIEW (e.g. an EARLY ultrasound scan, sometimes earlier than would be 'routine' for a patient WITHOUT this history) is OFTEN RECOMMENDED, GIVEN the INCREASED risk of RECURRENT ectopic pregnancy - this is NOT meant to cause ALARM, but represents PROACTIVE, INFORMED care planning\n→ DISCUSSING contraception (if NOT currently desiring pregnancy) should ALSO occur as part of COMPREHENSIVE discharge planning - returning to Module 1's point that NO method provides ABSOLUTE protection against ectopic pregnancy specifically, but EFFECTIVE contraception OVERALL remains important if AVOIDING pregnancy (of ANY type) is the GOAL at this time`,
        kp:[
          "Anti-D immunoglobulin should be given to Rh-negative patients with confirmed ectopic pregnancy, regardless of management approach - blood group/Rh status must be EXPLICITLY checked, especially in emergency presentations where this can be overlooked",
          "Follow-up varies by management type: methotrexate/salpingostomy/expectant management all require beta-hCG monitoring to resolution; salpingectomy generally doesn't require ongoing beta-hCG monitoring",
          "Ectopic pregnancy is BOTH a medical condition AND, for many patients, a pregnancy loss - these dimensions are experienced together even if discussed separately clinically",
          "Patients may experience grief, anxiety, guilt/self-blame (unwarranted), and - after emergency presentations - trauma processing needs; support should follow the PATIENT's lead on language/framing, not assumptions",
          "Future pregnancy planning includes early review/scan in future pregnancies (given recurrence risk) and discussion of contraception if pregnancy isn't currently desired"
        ],
        callouts:[
          {role:"student", type:"exam", text:"Anti-D administration is a FREQUENTLY TESTED 'don't forget' point precisely BECAUSE it can be OVERLOOKED amid OTHER priorities - a question might describe a COMPLETE, SUCCESSFUL management course (e.g. uncomplicated methotrexate treatment with beta-hCG resolving normally) and ask 'what ELSE should be checked/done before this patient's care is complete?' - testing whether you remember Rh status/Anti-D as a SEPARATE, ESSENTIAL step that's INDEPENDENT of the SUCCESS of the PRIMARY treatment."},
          {role:"doctor", type:"clinical", text:"Build CHECKING Rh STATUS and ADMINISTERING ANTI-D (if indicated) into STANDARDISED DISCHARGE CHECKLISTS for ectopic pregnancy, REGARDLESS of management type - relying on this being REMEMBERED 'in the moment,' particularly after an EMERGENCY presentation where the TEAM's attention has been on IMMEDIATE survival, is a RECOGNISED RISK for this step being MISSED. SYSTEM-LEVEL safeguards (checklists, prompts) support CONSISTENT delivery of this SIMPLE but IMPORTANT intervention."},
          {role:"pharmacist", type:"clinical", text:"Pharmacists involved in DISCHARGE MEDICATION REVIEWS for ectopic pregnancy patients can serve as an ADDITIONAL CHECKPOINT for confirming ANTI-D has been considered/administered if INDICATED (for Rh-negative patients) - this represents a PRACTICAL, MULTIDISCIPLINARY SAFETY NET, complementing medical/nursing checklists, particularly valuable given this step's tendency to be OVERLOOKED."},
          {role:"nurse", type:"clinical", text:"When providing DISCHARGE INFORMATION and EMOTIONAL SUPPORT, avoid ASSUMPTIONS about HOW a patient is feeling based on the CLINICAL SEVERITY of their presentation - a patient managed STRAIGHTFORWARDLY with EARLY medical management may STILL experience SIGNIFICANT grief/distress, while a patient who experienced a LIFE-THREATENING EMERGENCY may, at the point of discharge, be FOCUSED primarily on PHYSICAL recovery with EMOTIONAL processing occurring LATER - FOLLOWING THE PATIENT's CUES, and ENSURING they know SUPPORT REMAINS AVAILABLE AFTER discharge (not just 'in the moment'), is more helpful than ASSUMING a 'standard' emotional response."},
          {role:"labtech", type:"practical", text:"ENSURING Rh STATUS is CLEARLY DOCUMENTED and EASILY ACCESSIBLE to the CLINICAL TEAM (particularly relevant when results may have been generated DURING an EMERGENCY presentation, amid MULTIPLE other urgent results) supports the ANTI-D administration step discussed in this section - if Rh status results are 'BURIED' among OTHER emergency bloods without CLEAR FLAGGING, this INFORMATION may not be ACTED UPON before discharge, even though the RESULT itself was AVAILABLE."},
        ]
      },
    ],
    ev:"RCOG Green-top Guideline on Ectopic Pregnancy and Miscarriage; NICE Guideline NG126; WHO Recommendations on Maternal Health; RCOG Green-top Guideline on Massive Obstetric Haemorrhage; British Society for Haematology Guidelines on Anti-D Immunoglobulin"
  },
];

